WEBVTT

00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:05.280
Hi, I'm Bob Pringley and I'm here to tell you the incredible story about personal computers

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:06.280
to go over the world.

00:00:06.280 --> 00:00:08.620
Why am I telling you this in a basketball game?

00:00:08.620 --> 00:00:17.700
Well, I like the game, but mainly it's because of that guy down there.

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:22.400
His name is Paul Allen and everything you see here belongs to him.

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:28.540
The Portland Trailblazers basketball team, their arena, even the dancers.

00:00:28.540 --> 00:00:33.020
Thanks to personal computers he has eight billion dollars to spend on such toys.

00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:39.420
20 years ago, Allen and his high school friend Bill Gates were running a two-man software

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:41.620
company called Microsoft.

00:00:41.620 --> 00:00:47.620
Today, Allen is richer than God and Gates is richer than Allen.

00:00:47.620 --> 00:00:53.800
Twenty years ago, young men like Paul Allen and Bill Gates invented the personal computer

00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:58.480
and in doing so launched a revolution that's changed the way we live, work and communicate.

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:01.680
It's hard to believe that 20 years ago there were no personal computers.

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:07.180
Now it's the third largest industry in the world, somewhere between energy production and illegal drugs.

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:15.180
But the most amazing thing of all is that it happened by accident, because a bunch of disenfranchised nerds wanted to impress their friends.

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:55.960
This is the story of how a handful of guys launched an industrial revolution.

00:01:55.960 --> 00:01:58.460
How they changed the culture of business.

00:01:58.460 --> 00:02:00.460
How they made history.

00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:06.460
I feel incredibly lucky to be at exactly the right place in Silicon Valley

00:02:06.460 --> 00:02:13.460
at exactly the right time, historically, where this invention has taken form.

00:02:13.460 --> 00:02:16.460
It wasn't like we both thought it was going to go a long ways.

00:02:16.460 --> 00:02:21.460
It was like we both do it for fun, and even though we're going to lose some money, probably,

00:02:21.460 --> 00:02:24.460
we'll just have been able to say we had a company.

00:02:24.460 --> 00:02:30.460
All of us would get together and just hope we were right that the PC would become a big

00:02:30.460 --> 00:02:31.460
thing.

00:02:31.460 --> 00:02:37.180
You know I stopped and said wow, the PC really has become part of the very fabric of the way

00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:42.780
people live and we certainly search with it and I just stopped and said hmm, pretty

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:50.970
incredible ride.

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:55.170
Most of these people come from the place I call home, the Silicon Valley south of

00:02:55.170 --> 00:02:57.490
San Francisco, California.

00:02:57.490 --> 00:03:01.730
Coming up here near the electronics companies that give the place its name, these founders

00:03:01.730 --> 00:03:06.450
of the PC revolution were for the most part middle class white kids from good suburban

00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:07.450
homes.

00:03:07.450 --> 00:03:17.780
But it's not their homes we're interested in, it's their garages.

00:03:17.780 --> 00:03:21.220
This is my garage and this is all my junk.

00:03:21.220 --> 00:03:24.740
I'm probably one of the few guys in Silicon Valley who actually has room in his garage

00:03:24.740 --> 00:03:25.780
for a car.

00:03:25.780 --> 00:03:29.540
Most everyone else seems to use theirs to start computer companies and create great

00:03:29.540 --> 00:03:30.540
fortunes.

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:33.540
But I don't have a fortune. I'm a failure.

00:03:33.540 --> 00:03:39.540
I've written computer programs that almost ran and I've designed and built hardware devices that frankly didn't work at all.

00:03:39.540 --> 00:03:45.540
But I'm the ideal guy to tell the story of the personal computer business because I'm its premier gossip columnist.

00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:48.540
And everyone tells me all their secrets.

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:55.210
And this is my home where I write a gossip column for a computing magazine.

00:03:55.210 --> 00:03:57.210
Sorry about the mess.

00:03:57.210 --> 00:04:05.210
Institutions in constant change like the PC industry are driven by rumor and gossip, and I thrive on both.

00:04:05.210 --> 00:04:13.210
My electronic mail address is deluged with inside information about everything from product flaws to who's sleeping with whom.

00:04:13.210 --> 00:04:17.210
What ties these gossipers together is a desire for truth.

00:04:17.210 --> 00:04:21.210
These people and their love of technology have fueled the PC revolution.

00:04:21.210 --> 00:04:24.210
To understand them is to understand that revolution.

00:04:24.210 --> 00:04:26.210
So let's go find some.

00:04:26.210 --> 00:04:40.650
Meet Edwin Chin on a Saturday morning at the Weird Stuff warehouse.

00:04:40.650 --> 00:04:45.470
This could be 1976 or 1996,

00:04:45.470 --> 00:04:49.470
because there is always a new generation of techies like Edwin who hear the calling.

00:04:49.470 --> 00:04:53.470
Most other kids are watching TV, but not Edwin.

00:04:53.470 --> 00:05:01.470
You know, I'm interested in the electronics and technologies and the hobby since I started when I was like 6 or 7, you know.

00:05:01.470 --> 00:05:03.470
How old are you now, Edwin?

00:05:03.470 --> 00:05:05.470
10. Right now.

00:05:05.470 --> 00:05:10.980
It's no coincidence that the only woman in the vicinity looks bored.

00:05:10.980 --> 00:05:12.980
Because this is a boy thing.

00:05:12.980 --> 00:05:17.980
The obsession of a particular type of boy who would rather struggle with an electronic box

00:05:17.980 --> 00:05:19.980
than with a world of unpredictable people.

00:05:19.980 --> 00:05:22.980
We call them engineers, programmers, hackers and techies.

00:05:22.980 --> 00:05:25.980
But mainly, we call them nerds.

00:05:25.980 --> 00:05:31.980
I think a nerd is a person who uses the telephone to talk to other people about telephones.

00:05:31.980 --> 00:05:40.980
And a computer nerd, therefore, is somebody who uses a computer in order to use a computer.

00:05:40.980 --> 00:05:45.900
And people have different degrees of passion, different types of passion.

00:05:45.900 --> 00:05:49.900
Some people just live databases and it's just normal form.

00:05:49.900 --> 00:05:52.900
It's just like nirvana and they just quest for it.

00:05:52.900 --> 00:05:54.900
That's what gives them up in the morning.

00:05:54.900 --> 00:05:59.900
What do your friends think of you?

00:05:59.900 --> 00:06:01.900
Boy, he's a nerd.

00:06:01.900 --> 00:06:07.500
But I don't mind. I'm used to being called a nerd.

00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:10.500
You can't have other people stop your dreams.

00:06:10.500 --> 00:06:19.500
And in Silicon Valley, the dream is to grow up to become a boy like this.

00:06:19.500 --> 00:06:23.500
Graham Spencer is chief programmer for Architects Software.

00:06:23.500 --> 00:06:30.500
Six guys who graduated from Stanford University and started a company just because they like each other.

00:06:30.500 --> 00:06:34.810
This is a modern-day start-up,

00:06:34.810 --> 00:06:39.810
But at heart, it's no different from PC pioneers like Apple or Microsoft.

00:06:39.810 --> 00:06:41.810
Nerds who share a dream.

00:06:41.810 --> 00:06:48.810
Their hobby is their business and the culture they've created is identical to that of a thousand other technology companies.

00:06:48.810 --> 00:06:52.810
First, they dump the idea of 9 to 5.

00:06:52.810 --> 00:06:57.810
In this industry, you can work any 80 hours per week you like.

00:06:57.810 --> 00:07:03.810
And then I've got my camp, which I used to cover my eyes and sleep in the early morning while everybody's coming in.

00:07:03.810 --> 00:07:06.750
We didn't even obey a 24 hour clock.

00:07:06.750 --> 00:07:10.050
We'd come in and program for a couple days straight.

00:07:10.050 --> 00:07:13.250
We'd four or five of us when it was time to eat.

00:07:13.250 --> 00:07:15.850
We'd all get in our cars and kind of race over to the restaurant

00:07:15.850 --> 00:07:17.730
and sit and talk about what we were doing.

00:07:17.730 --> 00:07:20.690
Sometimes I get excited talking about things I forget to eat,

00:07:20.690 --> 00:07:23.970
but then we just go back and program some more.

00:07:23.970 --> 00:07:25.690
It was awesome, our friends.

00:07:25.690 --> 00:07:38.640
Those were fun days.

00:07:38.640 --> 00:07:41.040
Let's look in the refrigerator.

00:07:41.040 --> 00:07:45.000
Whoa, we have Coke and cold pizza.

00:07:45.000 --> 00:07:46.600
I do about two liters of Coke a day.

00:07:46.600 --> 00:07:47.440
I just...

00:07:47.440 --> 00:07:48.560
Two liters of Coke a day.

00:07:48.560 --> 00:07:51.680
And do you think of it like as brain food?

00:07:51.680 --> 00:07:54.200
I keep saying going, then listen to heavy metal

00:07:54.200 --> 00:08:01.360
and get caffeinated and hack.

00:08:01.360 --> 00:08:03.160
I'd sit down in my room on the floor

00:08:03.160 --> 00:08:04.760
with sheets of paper spread all around

00:08:04.760 --> 00:08:06.520
with my computer design I was working on

00:08:06.520 --> 00:08:09.120
and always I noticed that I was up pretty late at night

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:14.420
and I had lots of Cokes, just part of that life.

00:08:14.420 --> 00:08:21.420
combination of stale pizza and body odor and spilt cola kind of ground into the

00:08:21.420 --> 00:08:27.660
rug. I brought some spaghetti to work and then forgot to wash out the container

00:08:27.660 --> 00:08:32.820
for the last couple of days, maybe six or seven if I had to be honest. Oh that

00:08:32.820 --> 00:08:40.740
smells bad. Eating, bathing, having a girlfriend, having an act of social life.

00:08:40.740 --> 00:08:43.740
is it's a deathill, it gets in the way of code time.

00:08:43.740 --> 00:08:47.740
Writing code is the primary force that drives our lives.

00:08:47.740 --> 00:08:51.740
Anything that interrupts that is wasteful.

00:08:51.740 --> 00:08:59.710
What is it about the internal logic of a computer that's so enticing?

00:08:59.710 --> 00:09:03.710
For one thing, such logic can be understood,

00:09:03.710 --> 00:09:05.710
as opposed to things that can't be understood at all,

00:09:05.710 --> 00:09:09.710
like the motivations of young women say, or of the French.

00:09:09.710 --> 00:09:11.710
Let me explain.

00:09:11.710 --> 00:09:16.330
Time for the cringely crash course in basic computers.

00:09:16.330 --> 00:09:17.410
Part one.

00:09:17.410 --> 00:09:19.370
This is a mainframe computer.

00:09:19.370 --> 00:09:21.690
All of these cabinets are one machine.

00:09:21.690 --> 00:09:23.910
In the old days all computers were this size.

00:09:23.910 --> 00:09:28.090
They were tended by engineers and white coats, a kind of priest who took their jobs very

00:09:28.090 --> 00:09:29.090
seriously.

00:09:29.090 --> 00:09:31.430
Now all computers work pretty much the same.

00:09:31.430 --> 00:09:35.210
Whether it's a giant that serves 2,000 users like this one, or a little notebook

00:09:35.210 --> 00:09:36.810
that serves only me.

00:09:36.810 --> 00:09:40.810
They process numerical data, adding, multiplying, comparing.

00:09:40.810 --> 00:09:44.030
Fact is, if you can quantify it, a computer can handle it.

00:09:44.030 --> 00:09:45.410
It's the emotional stuff.

00:09:45.410 --> 00:09:47.290
They don't know what to do with.

00:09:47.290 --> 00:09:50.170
The data must be put into a special binary code

00:09:50.170 --> 00:09:52.930
consisting only of ones and zeros.

00:09:52.930 --> 00:09:55.330
And you have to give the computer instructions also

00:09:55.330 --> 00:09:58.250
in code to tell it exactly what to do with the data

00:09:58.250 --> 00:09:59.690
and in what order.

00:09:59.690 --> 00:10:02.450
These instructions are called a program.

00:10:02.450 --> 00:10:04.490
In the early days, you put in the instructions

00:10:04.490 --> 00:10:07.730
by flipping switches or loaded them from paper tape.

00:10:07.730 --> 00:10:10.170
This was called machine language.

00:10:10.170 --> 00:10:12.270
It made computers a pain to use.

00:10:12.270 --> 00:10:16.770
Even worse, every type of computer spoke a different machine language.

00:10:16.770 --> 00:10:22.270
While the ENIAC could compute the 30-second trajectory of a shell in 20 seconds,

00:10:22.270 --> 00:10:27.270
operators required two days to program it to do so.

00:10:27.270 --> 00:10:30.970
Then a US Navy captain named Grace Hopper solved the problem.

00:10:30.970 --> 00:10:33.270
She invented a computer language,

00:10:33.270 --> 00:10:37.270
English words that the computer itself could translate into binary code.

00:10:37.270 --> 00:10:43.830
Now users could type whole list of instructions into a computer rather than flipping those damn switches.

00:10:43.830 --> 00:10:47.110
Like most things having to do with computers, that first language had a silly name,

00:10:47.110 --> 00:10:50.710
COBOL. It was followed by other languages like FORTRAN and BASIC,

00:10:50.710 --> 00:10:53.670
and they all made computing just a bit more user friendly.

00:10:53.670 --> 00:10:58.470
So when some nerd tells you he's been up all night programming or writing software or hacking code,

00:10:58.470 --> 00:11:05.830
what he really means is he's been typing long list of instructions into his computer.

00:11:05.830 --> 00:11:08.470
Mainframe computers were far from personal.

00:11:08.470 --> 00:11:11.670
They sat in big air-conditioned rooms at insurance companies,

00:11:11.670 --> 00:11:13.390
phone companies, and the bank,

00:11:13.390 --> 00:11:15.590
and their main function was to get us confused

00:11:15.590 --> 00:11:17.590
with some other guy named Cringly,

00:11:17.590 --> 00:11:20.790
who was a deadbeat and had a criminal record.

00:11:20.790 --> 00:11:24.790
Eventually, computer terminals did begin to appear in some schools,

00:11:24.790 --> 00:11:26.910
but most of us paid no attention.

00:11:26.910 --> 00:11:29.670
But there was usually one kid who did pay attention,

00:11:29.670 --> 00:11:33.950
falling in love with the digital purity of those ones and zeros.

00:11:33.950 --> 00:11:35.230
He was the nerve.

00:11:35.230 --> 00:11:38.470
And I took this book home that described the PDPA computer,

00:11:38.470 --> 00:11:41.910
and it just, oh, it was just like a Bible to me.

00:11:41.910 --> 00:11:43.630
I mean, all these things that, for some reason,

00:11:43.630 --> 00:11:46.270
I'd fallen in love with, like you might fall in love with,

00:11:46.270 --> 00:11:48.190
a card game called Magic, or you might fall in love

00:11:48.190 --> 00:11:50.230
with doing crossword puzzles or something else,

00:11:50.230 --> 00:11:51.990
or playing a musical instrument.

00:11:51.990 --> 00:11:54.230
I fell in love with these little descriptions

00:11:54.230 --> 00:11:55.950
of computers on their inside.

00:11:55.950 --> 00:11:56.990
And it was a little mathematics.

00:11:56.990 --> 00:11:59.750
I could work out some problems on paper, and solve it,

00:11:59.750 --> 00:12:00.790
and see how it's done.

00:12:00.790 --> 00:12:02.190
And I could come up with my own solutions

00:12:02.190 --> 00:12:04.790
and feel good inside.

00:12:04.790 --> 00:12:07.090
So you would keyboard these commands in,

00:12:07.090 --> 00:12:08.450
and then you would wait for a while,

00:12:08.450 --> 00:12:10.590
and then the thing would go ta-ta-ta-ta-ta-ta-ta-ta-ta-ta-ta-ta-ta-ta

00:12:10.590 --> 00:12:12.750
and it would tell you something out.

00:12:12.750 --> 00:12:16.490
But even with that, it was still remarkable,

00:12:16.490 --> 00:12:17.890
especially for a 10-year-old,

00:12:17.890 --> 00:12:20.590
that you could write a program in basic, let's say,

00:12:20.590 --> 00:12:26.290
or Fortran, and actually this machine would sort of take

00:12:26.290 --> 00:12:33.790
your idea and it would sort of execute your idea

00:12:33.790 --> 00:12:35.790
and give you back some results.

00:12:35.790 --> 00:12:37.530
And if they were the results that you predicted,

00:12:37.530 --> 00:12:38.610
your program really worked.

00:12:38.610 --> 00:12:41.790
It was an incredibly thrilling experience.

00:12:41.790 --> 00:12:44.970
NERDS wanted their own computers right from the beginning,

00:12:44.970 --> 00:12:46.850
but it took a technological breakthrough

00:12:46.850 --> 00:12:48.930
to make that possible.

00:12:48.930 --> 00:12:52.250
This is it, the chip, the microprocessor.

00:12:52.250 --> 00:12:54.670
This is what allows you to have a mainframe computer

00:12:54.670 --> 00:12:55.970
on your desk.

00:12:55.970 --> 00:12:59.010
In the 1950s, mainframes were as big as this garage,

00:12:59.010 --> 00:13:00.850
and that's because they were filled with thousands

00:13:00.850 --> 00:13:03.530
of these, vacuum tubes or valves.

00:13:03.530 --> 00:13:07.530
Eventually the valves were made much smaller and replaced with transistors.

00:13:07.530 --> 00:13:11.030
Still too big, however, to make a computer that could fit on your desk.

00:13:11.030 --> 00:13:13.230
What that took was further miniaturization.

00:13:13.230 --> 00:13:17.130
Here we have a single piece of silicon etched with thousands of transistors.

00:13:17.130 --> 00:13:20.330
This microprocessor holds more than a million transistors.

00:13:20.330 --> 00:13:22.730
And that's the secret of the personal computer.

00:13:22.730 --> 00:13:29.870
And that's why they call it Silicon Valley, not Computer Valley.

00:13:29.870 --> 00:13:36.020
These are the people who invented the microprocessor, Intel.

00:13:36.020 --> 00:13:41.940
Intel was started 28 years ago by a handful of guys after a row with their old boss.

00:13:41.940 --> 00:13:46.500
Their microprocessors today power 85% of the world's computers.

00:13:46.500 --> 00:13:49.220
Are you a chess table?

00:13:49.220 --> 00:13:55.060
Intel not only invented the chip, they are responsible for the laid-back Silicon Valley working style.

00:13:55.060 --> 00:13:57.380
Everyone was on a first-name basis.

00:13:57.380 --> 00:14:01.620
There were no reserve parking places, no offices, only cubicles.

00:14:01.620 --> 00:14:03.460
It's still true today.

00:14:03.460 --> 00:14:05.960
Here's the chairman's cubicle.

00:14:05.960 --> 00:14:07.260
Knock, knock.

00:14:07.260 --> 00:14:09.760
I've knocked on the door, but there's no door.

00:14:09.760 --> 00:14:12.020
Gordon Moore is one of the Intel founders

00:14:12.020 --> 00:14:14.200
worth $3 billion.

00:14:14.200 --> 00:14:16.460
With money like that, I'd have a door.

00:14:16.460 --> 00:14:19.500
In a business like this, the people with the power

00:14:19.500 --> 00:14:21.260
are the ones that have the understanding

00:14:21.260 --> 00:14:24.200
of what's going on, not necessarily the ones on top.

00:14:24.200 --> 00:14:27.500
It's very important that those people that have the knowledge

00:14:27.500 --> 00:14:29.700
are the ones that make the decisions.

00:14:29.700 --> 00:14:32.700
So we set up something where everyone

00:14:32.700 --> 00:14:37.810
who had the knowledge at an equal state what was going on.

00:14:37.810 --> 00:14:41.210
Intel's microprocessors kept getting more powerful.

00:14:41.210 --> 00:14:44.310
They soon had enough horsepower to run a whole computer.

00:14:44.310 --> 00:14:47.810
Only Intel didn't appreciate the brilliance of their own product,

00:14:47.810 --> 00:14:52.510
seeing it as useful mainly for calculators or traffic lights.

00:14:52.510 --> 00:14:56.210
Intel had all the elements necessary to invent the PC business,

00:14:56.210 --> 00:14:59.310
but they just didn't get it.

00:14:59.310 --> 00:15:04.040
Lucky for us, someone did.

00:15:04.040 --> 00:15:14.500
This is the chip that launched the personal computer revolution.

00:15:14.500 --> 00:15:18.660
This is the magazine that announced it.

00:15:18.660 --> 00:15:26.260
In January 1975 featured on the cover was the world's first personal computer, the Altair 8800.

00:15:26.260 --> 00:15:32.260
It was the crazy idea of an ex-air force officer from Georgia, Ed Roberts.

00:15:32.260 --> 00:15:38.500
If you look at it, it was kind of grandiose, almost megalomaniac kind of scheme, you know.

00:15:38.500 --> 00:15:41.900
And right now I couldn't do it because I could see right off as though you could do this.

00:15:41.900 --> 00:15:44.700
I didn't know you could do this.

00:15:44.700 --> 00:15:50.700
But at that time, you know, we just lacked the benefits of age and experience.

00:15:50.700 --> 00:15:54.870
We didn't know we couldn't do it.

00:15:54.870 --> 00:15:58.070
Twenty years after Ed Roberts' flash of brilliance,

00:15:58.070 --> 00:16:02.870
this exhibit is being held to celebrate the anniversary of the outair.

00:16:02.870 --> 00:16:08.470
Like every other PC pioneer, Ed built his computer just because he wanted one to play with.

00:16:08.470 --> 00:16:12.950
There were some of us that lusted after computers really at that time.

00:16:12.950 --> 00:16:16.990
All the computers in the world tended to be in big centers and you had to get permission

00:16:16.990 --> 00:16:22.750
to get close to them and it was, you know, nobody had access to computers then.

00:16:22.750 --> 00:16:24.910
And the idea that you could have your own computer and do whatever you wanted to with

00:16:24.910 --> 00:16:33.110
it, whatever you wanted to, was fantastic.

00:16:33.110 --> 00:16:34.990
And where was all this happening?

00:16:34.990 --> 00:16:38.710
It was far from Silicon Valley, Intel or IBM.

00:16:38.710 --> 00:16:41.770
In the desert near the airport in Albuquerque, New Mexico,

00:16:41.770 --> 00:16:45.550
Ed Roberts ran a calculator company called MITS.

00:16:45.550 --> 00:16:48.310
Having an ugly building wasn't its only problem.

00:16:48.310 --> 00:16:50.250
MITS was going bankrupt.

00:16:50.250 --> 00:16:54.290
Nobody was buying calculators, and Ed needed $65,000

00:16:54.290 --> 00:16:55.890
just to stay afloat.

00:16:55.890 --> 00:16:57.530
We went to the buying.

00:16:57.530 --> 00:16:59.490
It had a late night meeting.

00:16:59.490 --> 00:17:01.830
And the issue was, will we close MITS down or keep?

00:17:01.830 --> 00:17:04.770
Are they loaned us an additional $65,000?

00:17:04.770 --> 00:17:06.170
And I was asked how many machines

00:17:06.170 --> 00:17:08.530
that I think we would sell in the next year

00:17:08.530 --> 00:17:13.810
after it was introduced. And I said 800. It was considered a wild-eyed optimist at that.

00:17:13.810 --> 00:17:17.150
We had a month after it was introduced where he'd get 250 orders a day.

00:17:17.150 --> 00:17:22.150
The Altair wasn't even a computer. It was a computer kit.

00:17:22.150 --> 00:17:26.150
Whoa, this is a pretty well-equipped machine.

00:17:26.150 --> 00:17:31.150
You had to build it yourself, and even then it usually didn't work.

00:17:31.150 --> 00:17:33.410
Still, the demand was amazing.

00:17:33.410 --> 00:17:38.410
And there were actually people that came to midst a couple people with camper trailers

00:17:38.410 --> 00:17:42.010
and camped out in the parking lot waiting for their machine.

00:17:42.010 --> 00:17:45.090
I mean, they were so eager.

00:17:45.090 --> 00:17:49.250
I mean, I think everybody had sort of daydreamed or Walter middied about owning a computer.

00:17:49.250 --> 00:17:54.250
The surprise was that it would be possible for the average college student, for example,

00:17:54.250 --> 00:17:59.570
who was living on bare subsistence, to actually buy a computer.

00:17:59.570 --> 00:18:03.410
This is what really amazed me was that people were so...

00:18:03.410 --> 00:18:07.530
There was a sort of a pent-up demand for having your own computer.

00:18:07.530 --> 00:18:17.290
And if it could be that cheap, what a wonderful thing.

00:18:17.290 --> 00:18:21.770
This is an Altair computer, the first personal computer.

00:18:21.770 --> 00:18:27.930
And not just any Altair, this is Altair serial number two, the second one made.

00:18:27.930 --> 00:18:32.290
The first Altair made was sent off to be photographed at a magazine and was lost

00:18:32.290 --> 00:18:33.290
in the mail.

00:18:33.290 --> 00:18:38.230
This is the oldest personal computer in the world.

00:18:38.230 --> 00:18:42.010
Pretty historic junk, but the question is, what do you do with it?

00:18:42.010 --> 00:18:46.210
I mean, it has a front panel with switches that you can click back and forth and some

00:18:46.210 --> 00:18:51.090
lights, but in the back, there's no place to connect a keyboard, there's no place to

00:18:51.090 --> 00:18:55.970
connect a monitor, there's no place to connect a printer.

00:18:55.970 --> 00:19:00.090
In fact, there's practically nothing at all that you can really do with this thing.

00:19:00.090 --> 00:19:11.460
But back then, 1975, the people who had it were thrilled.

00:19:11.460 --> 00:19:14.900
The nerds formed clubs to talk about their new toy.

00:19:14.900 --> 00:19:18.600
One of the first was the Homebrew Computer Club, which met on Wednesday evenings in a

00:19:18.600 --> 00:19:22.360
hall rented from Stanford University in Silicon Valley.

00:19:22.360 --> 00:19:27.200
Presiding over near Anarchy was Lee Felsenstein, who pretended to be in charge.

00:19:27.200 --> 00:19:32.440
I would start the meeting by making a horrendous loud noise because everyone was talking

00:19:32.440 --> 00:19:34.840
and I had to get some attention somehow.

00:19:34.840 --> 00:19:43.810
And I would use it to call on the person in question, make threatening gestures with it.

00:19:43.810 --> 00:19:47.210
Most of us were in the electronics industry to a certain extent.

00:19:47.210 --> 00:19:49.790
There was also a stratum of positions.

00:19:49.790 --> 00:19:53.730
And there were a lot of radio amateurs, for instance, finding a new technology that wasn't

00:19:53.730 --> 00:19:54.730
stale.

00:19:54.730 --> 00:20:00.570
But most of us were at a sort of middle level downwards.

00:20:00.570 --> 00:20:05.910
We saw ourselves as crazed, ignored geniuses or possibly geniuses.

00:20:05.910 --> 00:20:13.910
At least we could each hope to get our hands on a computer of our own.

00:20:13.910 --> 00:20:18.350
The very uselessness of the Altair is what drove the hobbyists together.

00:20:18.350 --> 00:20:21.910
Roger Mellon and Harry Garland started an early computer company.

00:20:21.910 --> 00:20:25.590
They came here to meet others and to figure out just what the heck could be done with

00:20:25.590 --> 00:20:26.910
this new toy.

00:20:26.910 --> 00:20:30.710
A solution in search of a problem.

00:20:30.710 --> 00:20:32.990
The Altair was tedious to use.

00:20:32.990 --> 00:20:37.350
At first, the only way that data and instructions could be given to the computer was by flipping

00:20:37.350 --> 00:20:38.590
switches.

00:20:38.590 --> 00:20:41.310
Take something trivial like 2 plus 2.

00:20:41.310 --> 00:20:43.630
Each 2 needed 8 different switches to be flipped.

00:20:43.630 --> 00:20:46.750
Then a 9th switch was used to load them all.

00:20:46.750 --> 00:20:48.830
ADD required another 9 switches.

00:20:48.830 --> 00:20:53.110
The answer 4 was if the 3rd light from the left turned on.

00:20:53.110 --> 00:20:54.110
Eureka!

00:20:54.110 --> 00:20:57.590
So if you had a program that was 100 bytes long, you had to go through this procedure

00:20:57.590 --> 00:20:59.630
100 times to load that into memory.

00:20:59.630 --> 00:21:01.510
It took a long time.

00:21:01.510 --> 00:21:05.770
I bet it did. And what happened if you lost power or you lost your way in the middle?

00:21:05.770 --> 00:21:08.170
You cried.

00:21:08.170 --> 00:21:13.070
The altair may have been frustrating, but it drove the nerds to experiment, finding real

00:21:13.070 --> 00:21:18.590
uses for the useless box, turning it from a curiosity to a computer.

00:21:18.590 --> 00:21:26.050
Steve Dompier set up an altair, laboriously keyed a program into it. Somebody knocked

00:21:26.050 --> 00:21:30.410
the plug out of the wall, and he had to do that all over again, but nobody knew

00:21:30.410 --> 00:21:32.210
what this was about.

00:21:32.210 --> 00:21:35.170
After all, was it just going to sit and flash its lights?

00:21:35.170 --> 00:21:35.810
No.

00:21:35.810 --> 00:21:39.250
You put a little transistor radio next to the altair,

00:21:39.250 --> 00:21:42.690
and he would, by manipulating the length of loops

00:21:42.690 --> 00:21:44.650
in the software, could play tunes.

00:21:44.650 --> 00:21:47.850
The radio began playing the fool on the hill.

00:21:47.850 --> 00:21:50.010
Da, da, da, da, da, da, da, da,

00:21:50.010 --> 00:21:53.330
in the tinny little tunes that you could tell were coming

00:21:53.330 --> 00:21:55.970
from the noise that the computer was generated,

00:21:55.970 --> 00:21:58.170
being picked up by the radio.

00:21:58.170 --> 00:22:00.930
everybody rose and applauded.

00:22:00.930 --> 00:22:02.770
I propose that he receive the

00:22:02.770 --> 00:22:04.890
stripped Phillips screw award for

00:22:04.890 --> 00:22:07.970
finding a use for something previously thought useless.

00:22:07.970 --> 00:22:11.210
But I think everyone was too busy applauding to even hear me.

00:22:11.210 --> 00:22:13.570
It was a very exciting thing.

00:22:13.570 --> 00:22:18.780
It was probably the first thing the Altair actually did.

00:22:18.780 --> 00:22:23.340
Turning the Altair into a useful tool required a programming language so

00:22:23.340 --> 00:22:25.620
users could type their programs in

00:22:25.620 --> 00:22:27.540
rather than flipping switches.

00:22:27.540 --> 00:22:33.240
What it needed was a version of some big computer language like BASIC, only modified for the PC.

00:22:33.240 --> 00:22:35.480
This was called a BASIC interpreter.

00:22:35.480 --> 00:22:43.200
But it didn't yet exist because the experts all thought that not even BASIC was BASIC enough to fit inside the tiny Altair memory.

00:22:43.200 --> 00:22:47.270
Yet again, the experts were wrong.

00:22:47.270 --> 00:22:49.790
Here comes the guy who solved the problem.

00:22:49.790 --> 00:22:56.750
Twenty years after finishing the first microcomputer BASIC, Paul Allen is returning to Albuquerque for a celebration of that event.

00:22:56.750 --> 00:23:03.520
this time with his $15 million jet and three-foot red carpet.

00:23:03.520 --> 00:23:05.920
At a time when I was killing brain cells,

00:23:05.920 --> 00:23:08.520
this guy was founding an empire.

00:23:08.520 --> 00:23:14.400
He has come to eat rubber chicken in honor of the Altair's

00:23:14.400 --> 00:23:16.160
20th anniversary.

00:23:16.160 --> 00:23:26.190
I'd like to introduce to you Paul Allen.

00:23:26.190 --> 00:23:28.850
Allen co-founded Microsoft with his younger buddy

00:23:28.850 --> 00:23:30.690
from high school, Bill Gates.

00:23:30.690 --> 00:23:33.290
One day in Boston I was in Harvard Square,

00:23:33.290 --> 00:23:35.890
and I covered popular products with this thing

00:23:35.890 --> 00:23:38.050
and it looked like what I had been imagining.

00:23:38.050 --> 00:23:40.410
And so I grabbed it off the shelf, I looked at it,

00:23:40.410 --> 00:23:43.170
and I bought it, and I ran back to Bill's dorm,

00:23:43.170 --> 00:23:45.450
and I think he was probably playing poker that night

00:23:45.450 --> 00:23:47.970
and usually losing money at that point.

00:23:49.130 --> 00:23:51.170
One of the few times when that's been the case.

00:23:51.170 --> 00:23:56.170
Paul showed that to me, then okay, here was a company

00:23:57.490 --> 00:23:59.450
that would be needing software.

00:23:59.450 --> 00:24:02.770
And he said, okay, well we gotta call these guys up

00:24:02.770 --> 00:24:04.170
and see if this thing is for real.

00:24:04.170 --> 00:24:06.450
we realized that things were starting to happen.

00:24:06.450 --> 00:24:09.490
And just because we'd had a vision for a long time

00:24:09.490 --> 00:24:12.770
of where this chip could go, what it could mean,

00:24:12.770 --> 00:24:14.690
that didn't mean the industry was going to wait for us

00:24:14.690 --> 00:24:18.250
while I stayed and finished my degree at Harvard.

00:24:18.250 --> 00:24:21.970
So called up and we've got this basic.

00:24:21.970 --> 00:24:24.210
It's just for your machines.

00:24:24.210 --> 00:24:25.890
It's not that far from being done.

00:24:25.890 --> 00:24:27.530
And we'd like to come out and show it to you.

00:24:27.530 --> 00:24:29.690
So we created this basic interpreter.

00:24:29.690 --> 00:24:34.010
Paul took the paper tape and flew out.

00:24:34.010 --> 00:24:38.650
In fact, the night before, he got some sleep while I double checked everything to make

00:24:38.650 --> 00:24:42.010
sure that we had it all right.

00:24:42.010 --> 00:24:45.170
But I had no idea what it was really going to be like to try to run the software.

00:24:45.170 --> 00:24:49.170
I had never been running an actual computer before.

00:24:49.170 --> 00:24:52.450
He was very nervous about whether this would actually work.

00:24:52.450 --> 00:24:58.010
And he got to the office and we all gathered around and he put his fingers on the switches

00:24:58.010 --> 00:25:02.690
and he loaded basic in with paper tape into the out there.

00:25:02.690 --> 00:25:04.690
I was just, I was so nervous.

00:25:04.690 --> 00:25:06.890
I just, this is just, it's not gonna work.

00:25:06.890 --> 00:25:07.730
It worked.

00:25:07.730 --> 00:25:11.250
And it came up, and it could do a few little simple things.

00:25:11.250 --> 00:25:12.810
And it was amazing when Paul called me up

00:25:12.810 --> 00:25:14.650
and said the thing had worked the first time.

00:25:14.650 --> 00:25:17.050
And of course it was incredibly fast.

00:25:17.050 --> 00:25:18.330
And it printed out memory size,

00:25:18.330 --> 00:25:20.410
and I think Bill said, well, it printed something.

00:25:20.410 --> 00:25:23.010
So I said, yeah, yeah.

00:25:23.010 --> 00:25:25.050
Oh, that was unbelievable.

00:25:25.050 --> 00:25:29.810
The fact that it really worked was a breakthrough.

00:25:29.810 --> 00:25:32.170
Maybe there wouldn't be a Microsoft if it hadn't,

00:25:32.170 --> 00:25:34.770
If the screen hadn't come alive, who knows?

00:25:34.770 --> 00:25:43.120
It might all be quite different.

00:25:43.120 --> 00:25:45.320
After the demo succeeded, Bill forgot

00:25:45.320 --> 00:25:47.320
about finishing university.

00:25:47.320 --> 00:25:50.240
Afraid of missing his chance to dominate the new industry,

00:25:50.240 --> 00:25:52.880
he joined Allen in what was then the center of world

00:25:52.880 --> 00:25:55.720
microcomputing research, among the sleazy bars

00:25:55.720 --> 00:26:02.120
and gas stations of Albuquerque, New Mexico.

00:26:02.120 --> 00:26:03.440
And they lived across the street

00:26:03.440 --> 00:26:06.640
from Mitz in the Sundowner Motel

00:26:06.640 --> 00:26:09.240
and the Altaire computer.

00:26:09.240 --> 00:26:14.120
and gradually they actually started Microsoft here in Albuquerque.

00:26:14.120 --> 00:26:18.520
We hired some of our high school friends basically to come down

00:26:18.520 --> 00:26:23.240
and stay with us in our apartment which became very crowded.

00:26:23.240 --> 00:26:27.160
We were pretty young. We started when I was 19

00:26:27.160 --> 00:26:29.920
and so we just had a lot of energy.

00:26:29.920 --> 00:26:31.560
They worked really hard.

00:26:31.560 --> 00:26:34.680
They listened to really loud music.

00:26:34.680 --> 00:26:37.200
I could hardly stand to go to the software room sometimes

00:26:37.200 --> 00:26:39.920
because the music would be banging off the walls,

00:26:39.920 --> 00:26:44.770
mostly acid rock.

00:26:44.770 --> 00:26:47.570
You know, we'd usually go out, eat pizzas,

00:26:47.570 --> 00:26:55.340
and then go out and watch action movies.

00:26:55.340 --> 00:26:57.220
They would work all night long.

00:26:57.220 --> 00:26:59.540
And there were days when Bill Gates would be sleeping

00:26:59.540 --> 00:27:02.180
on the floor in the software lab.

00:27:02.180 --> 00:27:05.260
And sometimes it would be buildings to other guys,

00:27:05.260 --> 00:27:09.260
all sitting on tables around the apartment with stacks

00:27:09.260 --> 00:27:10.620
of stacks of paper, right?

00:27:10.620 --> 00:27:13.620
converting the basic for the 8080.

00:27:13.620 --> 00:27:16.380
I still hold source code by heart.

00:27:16.380 --> 00:27:19.620
That was a work of love.

00:27:19.620 --> 00:27:22.340
And we just kept tuning and tuning that thing.

00:27:22.340 --> 00:27:30.060
And so that kind of craftsmanship paid off.

00:27:30.060 --> 00:27:33.420
Basic let the out there be used for both fun stuff

00:27:33.420 --> 00:27:34.860
and real work.

00:27:34.860 --> 00:27:36.900
People attached terminals to the computer

00:27:36.900 --> 00:27:39.260
and began writing games, word processors,

00:27:39.260 --> 00:27:41.420
and accounting programs.

00:27:41.420 --> 00:27:43.660
Most of us didn't notice, but soon there

00:27:43.660 --> 00:27:46.380
was a thriving industry for enthusiasts.

00:27:46.380 --> 00:27:49.740
By the end of 1975, dozens of other companies

00:27:49.740 --> 00:27:53.060
were building microcomputers.

00:27:53.060 --> 00:27:54.300
We created an industry.

00:27:54.300 --> 00:27:56.460
And I think that goes completely unnoticed.

00:27:56.460 --> 00:27:57.420
I mean, there was nothing.

00:27:57.420 --> 00:27:59.820
Every aspect of the industry, we talked about software,

00:27:59.820 --> 00:28:02.940
hardware, applications, stuff, dealerships, you name it,

00:28:02.940 --> 00:28:03.980
was all done at mess.

00:28:03.980 --> 00:28:05.020
It was a wild time.

00:28:05.020 --> 00:28:07.140
It was a very exciting time.

00:28:07.140 --> 00:28:09.940
And the first user convention,

00:28:09.940 --> 00:28:11.900
where we got people to come in and tell us

00:28:11.900 --> 00:28:16.660
what they were doing, what they were excited about, and other companies like

00:28:16.660 --> 00:28:21.940
processor technology or MSI or KREMEMCO got going as add-on companies.

00:28:21.940 --> 00:28:26.500
These companies are long forgotten, but they were the humble beginnings of the

00:28:26.500 --> 00:28:29.540
of the PC industry.

00:28:29.540 --> 00:28:33.540
Left in the hands of those early hobbyists, the PC might have never made it

00:28:33.540 --> 00:28:35.380
to the shopping mall.

00:28:35.380 --> 00:28:39.340
Reaching the wider market required a different type of vision.

00:28:39.340 --> 00:28:41.740
enter the flower children of California,

00:28:41.740 --> 00:28:48.290
who thought the PC was, well, groovy.

00:28:48.290 --> 00:28:52.770
From the safety of secret committees,

00:28:52.770 --> 00:28:55.890
they talked about the danger of war.

00:28:55.890 --> 00:28:58.330
Remember that the 60s happened in the early 70s, right?

00:28:58.330 --> 00:28:58.970
We have to remember that.

00:28:58.970 --> 00:29:00.450
And that's sort of when I came of age.

00:29:00.450 --> 00:29:01.610
So I saw a lot of this.

00:29:01.610 --> 00:29:06.650
And to me, the spark of that was

00:29:06.650 --> 00:29:11.450
that there was something beyond sort

00:29:11.450 --> 00:29:12.990
of what you see every day.

00:29:12.990 --> 00:29:16.150
Far from the smell of the gun.

00:29:16.150 --> 00:29:17.810
It's the same thing that causes people

00:29:17.810 --> 00:29:21.470
to want to be poets instead of bankers.

00:29:21.470 --> 00:29:24.010
And I think that's a wonderful thing.

00:29:24.010 --> 00:29:29.130
And I think that that same spirit can be put into products.

00:29:29.130 --> 00:29:31.250
And those products can be manufactured and given

00:29:31.250 --> 00:29:33.090
to people, and they can sense that spirit.

00:29:33.090 --> 00:29:36.570
And a whole bunch of us had the same community spirit.

00:29:36.570 --> 00:29:39.930
And that permeated the whole Humberer Computer Club.

00:29:39.930 --> 00:29:41.930
As soon as somebody would solve a problem,

00:29:41.930 --> 00:29:44.010
they'd come running down to the Homebrew Computer Club's

00:29:44.010 --> 00:29:46.290
next meeting, say, hey, everybody, you know that problem

00:29:46.290 --> 00:29:48.570
that all of us have been trying to figure out how to solve?

00:29:48.570 --> 00:29:49.650
Here's the solution.

00:29:49.650 --> 00:29:50.650
Isn't this wonderful?

00:29:50.650 --> 00:29:52.050
Our high, great guy.

00:29:52.050 --> 00:29:53.650
And it's my contention that that

00:29:53.650 --> 00:29:56.450
is a major component of why Silicon Valley was

00:29:56.450 --> 00:29:59.530
able to develop the technologies rapidly as it did,

00:29:59.530 --> 00:30:01.370
because we were all sharing.

00:30:01.370 --> 00:30:04.610
Everybody won.

00:30:04.610 --> 00:30:08.170
Out of this creative show and tell came Apple Computer,

00:30:08.170 --> 00:30:10.890
the first mass market PC company.

00:30:10.890 --> 00:30:13.250
The Apple founders, a couple of recent graduates

00:30:13.250 --> 00:30:16.610
from Homestead High, were regulars at homebrew meetings.

00:30:16.610 --> 00:30:18.930
Steve Wozniak was the technical wizard,

00:30:18.930 --> 00:30:20.650
and Steve Jobs was the visionary

00:30:20.650 --> 00:30:23.570
who saw microcomputers as a possible business.

00:30:24.850 --> 00:30:27.610
The first Apple computer was primitive.

00:30:27.610 --> 00:30:29.110
It was cobbled together by Woz

00:30:29.110 --> 00:30:31.890
to impress his friends at the homebrew meetings.

00:30:31.890 --> 00:30:33.130
Everybody was interested in computers,

00:30:33.130 --> 00:30:35.110
so I started getting a crowd around me,

00:30:35.110 --> 00:30:36.890
because even though I was too shy to raise my hand

00:30:36.890 --> 00:30:41.450
say anything in a club meeting. After the club meetings, I would put my computer that

00:30:41.450 --> 00:30:45.890
I had built and every week it had a little bit more working on it too. But I would set

00:30:45.890 --> 00:30:49.210
it down and let people type on the keyboard, I would explain what's in it. If they come

00:30:49.210 --> 00:30:54.570
up to me and ask a question, I can answer. Nowadays, I would have the ability to tell

00:30:54.570 --> 00:30:57.890
them what it is and be a little bit more promotional, but back then I could only

00:30:57.890 --> 00:31:01.370
answer questions if they asked me. But I got a group that started gathering around

00:31:01.370 --> 00:31:05.050
me and State Jobs saw that I had a lot of interest around me at the club and

00:31:05.050 --> 00:31:11.490
said let's start selling it and let's make this company came up with the name Apple and

00:31:11.490 --> 00:31:17.800
and how it started.

00:31:17.800 --> 00:31:22.880
Apple was at best a funky company started by a couple of teenage hackers who previously

00:31:22.880 --> 00:31:27.560
had been working as Alice in Wonderland characters at a local shopping mall and they started

00:31:27.560 --> 00:31:29.960
it in this garage right here.

00:31:29.960 --> 00:31:33.360
The first Apple computer was built here now there are more than 10 million in use

00:31:33.360 --> 00:31:37.720
around the world and I was there well for a short time I was an employee at Apple

00:31:37.720 --> 00:31:42.760
computer, employee number 12, and one day I helped move materials out of this garage.

00:31:42.760 --> 00:31:48.040
At the time, Steve Jobs said the company was short of loot, so he offered to pay me in company

00:31:48.040 --> 00:31:50.880
shares, but I held out for the money.

00:31:50.880 --> 00:31:55.030
My mother still reminds me of that incident.

00:31:55.030 --> 00:31:59.310
The Apple One was even less of a computer than the Altair.

00:31:59.310 --> 00:32:03.710
A single circuit board that came with neither a case nor a keyboard.

00:32:03.710 --> 00:32:07.750
Still, Steve Jobs managed to sell 50 Apple Ones.

