00:47:59.350 --> 00:48:01.210
every day seemed like Christmas,

00:48:01.210 --> 00:48:03.310
but Scrooge was around the corner.

00:48:03.310 --> 00:48:05.270
There was a company that everyone associated

00:48:05.270 --> 00:48:06.870
with the word computer,

00:48:06.870 --> 00:48:08.030
a company that expected,

00:48:08.030 --> 00:48:11.950
no demanded to dominate its market, IBM.

00:48:11.950 --> 00:48:13.830
Big Blue was on the move

00:48:13.830 --> 00:49:13.940
and Silicon Valley would soon be feeling the reverberations.

00:49:13.940 --> 00:49:15.740
The story so far.

00:49:15.740 --> 00:49:20.180
In 1975, Ed Roberts invented the Altair personal computer.

00:49:20.180 --> 00:49:23.100
It was a pain to use until 19-year-old pre-billionaire

00:49:23.100 --> 00:49:26.300
Bill Gates wrote the first personal computer language.

00:49:26.300 --> 00:49:28.540
Still, the public didn't care.

00:49:28.540 --> 00:49:32.020
Then two young hackers, Steve Jobs and Steve Wozniak,

00:49:32.020 --> 00:49:35.020
built the Apple computer to impress their friends.

00:49:35.020 --> 00:49:38.620
We were all impressed, and Apple was a stunning success.

00:49:38.620 --> 00:49:42.820
By 1980, the PC market was worth a billion dollars.

00:49:42.820 --> 00:50:14.310
Now, view on.

00:50:14.310 --> 00:50:15.150
Our nerds.

00:50:16.430 --> 00:50:18.470
Most of the people in history were young

00:50:18.470 --> 00:50:21.550
because the guys who had any real experience

00:50:21.550 --> 00:50:22.790
were too smart to get involved

00:50:22.790 --> 00:50:24.630
in all these crazy little machines.

00:50:25.950 --> 00:50:27.950
It really wasn't that we were gonna build

00:50:27.950 --> 00:50:29.390
billion dollar businesses.

00:50:29.390 --> 00:50:30.910
We were having a good time.

00:50:32.350 --> 00:50:34.350
I thought this was the most fun you could possibly have

00:50:34.350 --> 00:50:35.350
as you close on.

00:50:35.350 --> 00:50:39.710
When the personal computer was invented more than 20 years ago it was just that

00:50:39.710 --> 00:50:45.670
an invention it wasn't a business these were hobbyists who built these machines

00:50:45.670 --> 00:50:51.950
and wrote this software to have fun but that has really changed and now this is

00:50:51.950 --> 00:50:55.750
a business this is a big business.

00:50:55.750 --> 00:51:15.730
How the personal computer industry

00:51:15.730 --> 00:51:20.730
history grew from zero to 100 million units is an amazing story.

00:51:20.730 --> 00:51:26.730
And it wasn't just those early, funky companies of nerds and hackers like Apple that made it happen.

00:51:26.730 --> 00:51:31.730
Most of this transformation from hobby to big business can be linked to three letters.

00:51:31.730 --> 00:51:36.230
IBM.

00:51:36.230 --> 00:51:40.230
IBM was and is an American business phenomenon.

00:51:40.230 --> 00:51:45.230
Over 60 years, Tom Watson and his son, Tom Jr., built what their workers called Big Blue

00:51:45.230 --> 00:51:48.030
into the top computer company in the world.

00:51:48.030 --> 00:51:50.910
But IBM made mainframe computers for large companies,

00:51:50.910 --> 00:51:53.710
not personal computers, at least not yet.

00:51:53.710 --> 00:51:56.510
For the PC to be taken seriously by big business,

00:51:56.510 --> 00:51:59.210
the nerds of Silicon Valley had to meet the suits

00:51:59.210 --> 00:52:05.130
of corporate America.

00:52:05.130 --> 00:52:09.070
IBM never fired anyone requiring only undying loyalty

00:52:09.070 --> 00:52:13.380
to the company and the strict dress code.

00:52:13.380 --> 00:52:17.020
IBM hired conservative hard workers straight from school.

00:52:17.020 --> 00:52:22.140
Few IBMers were at the summer of love.

00:52:22.140 --> 00:52:24.300
Their turn-ons were giant mainframes

00:52:24.300 --> 00:52:26.180
and corporate responsibility.

00:52:26.180 --> 00:52:27.340
They worked nine to five,

00:52:27.340 --> 00:52:40.850
and on Saturdays washed the car.

00:52:40.850 --> 00:52:43.210
This is intergalactic HQ for IBM,

00:52:43.210 --> 00:52:45.250
the largest computer company in the world.

00:52:45.250 --> 00:52:47.690
But in many ways, IBM is really more a country

00:52:47.690 --> 00:52:49.090
than it is a company.

00:52:49.090 --> 00:52:50.930
It has hundreds of thousands of citizens.

00:52:50.930 --> 00:52:52.290
It has a bureaucracy.

00:52:52.290 --> 00:52:53.930
It has an entire culture.

00:52:53.930 --> 00:52:59.660
Everything, in fact, but an army.

00:52:59.660 --> 00:53:01.900
Okay Sam, we're ready to visit IBM country.

00:53:01.900 --> 00:53:03.900
Obviously, we're dressed for the part.

00:53:03.900 --> 00:53:07.060
Now, when you were in sales training in 1959 for IBM,

00:53:07.060 --> 00:53:08.460
did you sing company songs?

00:53:08.460 --> 00:53:09.260
Absolutely.

00:53:09.260 --> 00:53:11.020
Well, just to get us in the mood,

00:53:11.020 --> 00:53:12.260
let's sing one right here.

00:53:12.260 --> 00:53:16.300
I have the IBM, the songs of the IBM,

00:53:16.300 --> 00:53:18.340
and we're gonna try for number 74,

00:53:18.340 --> 00:53:22.660
our IBM salesman sung to the tune of Jingle Bells.

00:53:22.660 --> 00:53:26.740
IBM, happy men smiling all the way.

00:53:26.740 --> 00:53:30.940
Oh what fun it is to sell our products night and day

00:53:30.940 --> 00:53:34.940
IBM Watson men, partners of TJ

00:53:34.940 --> 00:53:39.740
In his service to mankind that's why we are so gay

00:53:39.740 --> 00:53:42.340
Now gay didn't mean what it means today then

00:53:42.340 --> 00:53:43.340
Remember that

00:53:43.340 --> 00:53:44.340
Right

00:53:44.340 --> 00:53:45.340
Okay, let's go

00:53:45.340 --> 00:53:46.340
Okay

00:53:46.340 --> 00:53:47.340
I guess it was okay

00:53:47.340 --> 00:53:48.340
Perfect

00:53:48.340 --> 00:53:49.340
Yeah

00:53:49.340 --> 00:53:56.790
When I started at IBM there was a dress code

00:53:56.790 --> 00:54:00.790
That was an informal oral code of white shirts.

00:54:00.790 --> 00:54:03.230
You couldn't wear anything but a white shirt,

00:54:03.230 --> 00:54:05.270
generally with a starch collar.

00:54:05.270 --> 00:54:08.590
I remember attending my first class,

00:54:08.590 --> 00:54:11.830
and a gentleman said to me as we were entering the building,

00:54:11.830 --> 00:54:12.790
are you an IVMmer?

00:54:12.790 --> 00:54:14.230
And I said, yes.

00:54:14.230 --> 00:54:18.750
He had a three-piece suit on, vest were of the Vogue.

00:54:18.750 --> 00:54:22.470
And he said, could you just lift your pants leg, please?

00:54:22.470 --> 00:54:23.670
I said, what?

00:54:23.670 --> 00:54:26.670
And before I knew it, he had lifted my pants

00:54:26.670 --> 00:54:29.570
and he said, you're not wearing any garters.

00:54:29.570 --> 00:54:31.570
I said, what?

00:54:31.570 --> 00:54:33.070
He said, you're socks.

00:54:33.070 --> 00:54:34.710
You're not pulled tight to the top.

00:54:34.710 --> 00:54:36.470
You need garters.

00:54:36.470 --> 00:54:41.900
And sure enough, I had to go get garters.

00:54:41.900 --> 00:54:45.100
IVM is like Switzerland, conservative, a little dull,

00:54:45.100 --> 00:54:46.300
yet prosperous.

00:54:46.300 --> 00:54:48.780
It has committees to verify each decision.

00:54:48.780 --> 00:54:50.420
The safety net is so big that it's

00:54:50.420 --> 00:54:52.820
hard to make a bad decision or any decision at all.

00:54:52.820 --> 00:54:56.420
At which we announced a new organizational structure.

00:54:56.420 --> 00:55:02.020
Rich Seidner, computer programmer and wannabe Paul Simon spent 25 years marching in step

00:55:02.020 --> 00:55:03.540
at IBM.

00:55:03.540 --> 00:55:04.540
He feels better now.

00:55:04.540 --> 00:55:11.500
I mean it's like getting 400,000 people to agree what they want to have for lunch.

00:55:11.500 --> 00:55:14.260
You know, I mean it's just not going to, it's going to be lowest common thing, you

00:55:14.260 --> 00:55:16.660
know, it's going to be hot dogs and beans.

00:55:16.660 --> 00:55:19.660
So what are you going to do?

00:55:19.660 --> 00:55:24.780
So IBM had created this process and absolutely made sure that quality would be preserved

00:55:24.780 --> 00:55:28.540
throughout the process that you actually were doing what you set out to do and what

00:55:28.540 --> 00:55:33.600
you thought the customer wanted. At one point, somebody kind of looked at the process to

00:55:33.600 --> 00:55:37.620
see, well, you know, what's it doing and what's the overhead built into it. What they

00:55:37.620 --> 00:55:43.980
found is that it would take at least nine months to ship an empty box.

00:55:43.980 --> 00:55:48.740
By the late 70s, even IBM had begun to notice the explosive growth of personal computer

00:55:48.740 --> 00:55:55.740
companies like Apple. The Apple II small inexpensive simple to use. The first computer

00:55:55.740 --> 00:56:01.300
What's more it was a computer business they didn't control. In 1980 IBM decided they wanted

00:56:01.300 --> 00:56:09.020
a piece of this action. There were suddenly tens of thousands of people buying machines

00:56:09.020 --> 00:56:13.100
of that class and they loved them and they were very happy with them and they were showing

00:56:13.100 --> 00:56:19.540
up in the engineering departments of our clients as machines that were brought in because you

00:56:19.540 --> 00:56:22.260
can't do the job on your mainframe kind of thing.

00:56:22.260 --> 00:56:27.780
J.B. wanted to know why I'm doing better than all the other managers. It's no secret

00:56:27.780 --> 00:56:33.140
I have an apple. Sure there's a big computer three flights down but I won't test my options.

00:56:33.140 --> 00:56:36.340
Do my charts or edit my reports like my apple.

00:56:36.340 --> 00:56:42.100
People who gotten it were religious fanatics about them. So the concern was we were

00:56:42.100 --> 00:56:50.560
losing the hearts and minds and give me a machine to win back the hearts and minds.

00:56:50.560 --> 00:56:55.160
In business as in comedy timing is everything and time looked like it might be running out

00:56:55.160 --> 00:57:05.040
for an IBM PC. I'm visiting the IBMer who took up the challenge. In August 1979 as IBM's

00:57:05.680 --> 00:57:10.520
top management meant to discuss their PC crisis Bill Low ran a small lab in Boca

00:57:10.520 --> 00:57:12.520
return to Florida.

00:57:12.520 --> 00:57:14.520
Hey, nice to see you again.

00:57:14.520 --> 00:57:16.520
I tried to match the IBM test code.

00:57:16.520 --> 00:57:18.520
That's terrific.

00:57:18.520 --> 00:57:20.520
He knew the company was in a quandary.

00:57:20.520 --> 00:57:22.520
Wait another year and the PC industry

00:57:22.520 --> 00:57:24.520
would be too big even for IBM to take on.

00:57:26.520 --> 00:57:28.520
Chairman Frank Carey turned

00:57:28.520 --> 00:57:30.520
to the department heads and said,

00:57:30.520 --> 00:57:32.520
Help!

00:57:32.520 --> 00:57:34.520
He kind of said, well, what should we do?

00:57:34.520 --> 00:57:36.520
And I said, well,

00:57:36.520 --> 00:57:38.520
we think we know what we

00:57:38.520 --> 00:57:41.480
we would like to do if we were going to proceed with our own product.

00:57:41.480 --> 00:57:42.800
And he said, no.

00:57:42.800 --> 00:57:46.520
He said, at IBM, it would take four years and 300 people

00:57:46.520 --> 00:57:47.360
to do anything.

00:57:47.360 --> 00:57:49.440
I mean, it's just a fact of life.

00:57:49.440 --> 00:57:53.760
And I said, no, sir, we can provide you a product in a year.

00:57:53.760 --> 00:57:55.760
And he abruptly ended the meeting.

00:57:55.760 --> 00:57:56.760
He said, you're on loan.

00:57:56.760 --> 00:57:59.560
Come back in two weeks and tell me what you need.

00:57:59.560 --> 00:58:01.640
An IBM product in a year?

00:58:01.640 --> 00:58:03.160
Ridiculous.

00:58:03.160 --> 00:58:06.200
Down in the basement, Bill still has the plan

00:58:06.200 --> 00:58:09.520
To save time, instead of building a computer from scratch,

00:58:09.520 --> 00:58:12.560
they would buy components off the shelf and assemble them.

00:58:12.560 --> 00:58:17.640
What in IBM speak was called open architecture.

00:58:17.640 --> 00:58:19.640
IBM never did this.

00:58:19.640 --> 00:58:23.360
Two weeks later, Bill proposed his heresy to the chairman.

00:58:23.360 --> 00:58:26.120
And frankly, this is it.

00:58:26.120 --> 00:58:31.920
The key decisions were to go with an open architecture,

00:58:31.920 --> 00:58:39.720
non-IBM technology, non-IBM software, non-IBM sales, and non-IBM service.

00:58:39.720 --> 00:58:47.080
And we probably spent a full half of the presentation carrying the corporate management committee

00:58:47.080 --> 00:58:51.480
into this concept, because this was a new concept for IBM at that point.

00:58:51.480 --> 00:58:55.040
Was it a hard sell?

00:58:55.040 --> 00:59:02.520
Kerry bought it. And as a result of him buying it, we got through it.

00:59:02.520 --> 00:59:06.880
With the backing of the chairman, Bill and his team then set out to break all the IBM

00:59:06.880 --> 00:59:11.880
rules and go for a record.

00:59:11.880 --> 00:59:16.760
Once IBM had decided to do a personal computer and to do it in a year, they couldn't really

00:59:16.760 --> 00:59:21.040
design anything. They just had to slap it together. So that's what we'll do. You

00:59:21.040 --> 00:59:22.600
We have a central processing unit.

00:59:22.600 --> 00:59:24.840
And let's see.

00:59:24.840 --> 00:59:32.600
You need a monitor or display and a keyboard.

00:59:32.600 --> 00:59:35.480
OK, a PC, except it's not.

00:59:35.480 --> 00:59:38.320
There's something missing.

00:59:38.320 --> 00:59:42.640
Time for the cringely crash course and elementary computing.

00:59:42.640 --> 00:59:45.520
A PC is a box full of electronic switches,

00:59:45.520 --> 00:59:46.360
a piece of hardware.

00:59:46.360 --> 00:59:48.760
It's useless until you tell it what to do.

00:59:48.760 --> 00:59:50.920
It requires a program of instructions.

00:59:50.920 --> 00:59:52.360
That's software.

00:59:52.360 --> 00:59:55.520
Every PC requires at least two essential bits of software

00:59:55.520 --> 00:59:56.960
in order to work at all.

00:59:56.960 --> 00:59:59.320
First, it requires a computer language.

00:59:59.320 --> 01:00:01.840
That's what you type in to give instructions

01:00:01.840 --> 01:00:02.880
to the computer.

01:00:02.880 --> 01:00:04.160
Tell it what to do.

01:00:04.160 --> 01:00:06.080
Remember it was a computer language called basic

01:00:06.080 --> 01:00:08.320
that Paul Allen and Bill Gates adapted to the Altair,

01:00:08.320 --> 01:00:09.720
the first PC.

01:00:09.720 --> 01:00:11.200
The other bit of software that's required

01:00:11.200 --> 01:00:12.480
is called an operating system.

01:00:12.480 --> 01:00:15.320
That's the internal traffic cop that tells the computer

01:00:15.320 --> 01:00:18.840
itself how the keyboard is connected to the screen

01:00:18.840 --> 01:00:21.280
or how to store files on a floppy disk

01:00:21.280 --> 01:00:23.520
instead of just losing them when you turn off the PC

01:00:23.520 --> 01:00:25.280
at the end of the day.

01:00:25.280 --> 01:00:27.960
Operating systems tend to have boring, unfriendly names

01:00:27.960 --> 01:00:31.560
like UNIX and CPM and MS-DOS.

01:00:31.560 --> 01:00:33.240
But though they may be boring, it's

01:00:33.240 --> 01:00:35.960
an operating system that made Bill Gates the richest man

01:00:35.960 --> 01:00:37.360
in the world.

01:00:37.360 --> 01:00:38.760
And the story of how that came about

01:00:38.760 --> 01:00:42.870
is, well, pretty interesting.

01:00:42.870 --> 01:00:45.030
So the contest begins.

01:00:45.030 --> 01:00:48.150
Who would IBM buy their software from?

01:00:48.150 --> 01:00:50.050
Let's meet the two contenders,

01:00:50.050 --> 01:00:55.110
the late Gary Kildall then age 39, a computer science PhD,

01:00:55.110 --> 01:01:00.810
and a 24-year-old Harvard dropout, Bill Gates.

01:01:00.810 --> 01:01:03.930
By the time IBM came calling in 1980,

01:01:03.930 --> 01:01:06.290
Bill Gates and his small company, Microsoft,

01:01:06.290 --> 01:01:08.810
was the biggest supplier of computer languages

01:01:08.810 --> 01:01:11.930
in the fledgling PC industry.

01:01:11.930 --> 01:01:14.050
Many different computer manufacturers

01:01:14.050 --> 01:01:18.010
are making the CPM operating system standard on most models.

01:01:18.010 --> 01:01:20.130
For their operating system, though,

01:01:20.130 --> 01:01:23.810
The logical guy for the IBM is to see was Gary Kildall.

01:01:23.810 --> 01:01:28.130
He ran a company modestly called Intergalactic Digital Research.

01:01:28.130 --> 01:01:32.290
Gary had invented the PC's first operating system called CPM.

01:01:32.290 --> 01:01:34.650
He had already sold 600,000 of them.

01:01:34.650 --> 01:01:37.730
So he was the big cheese of operating systems.

01:01:37.730 --> 01:01:41.010
In the early 70s, I had a need for an operating system myself.

01:01:41.010 --> 01:01:43.770
And it was a very natural thing to write.

01:01:43.770 --> 01:01:46.290
And it turns out other people had a need for an operating system

01:01:46.290 --> 01:01:47.170
like that.

01:01:47.170 --> 01:01:49.250
And so it was a very natural thing.

01:01:49.250 --> 01:01:52.130
wrote it for my own use and then started selling it.

01:01:52.130 --> 01:01:56.530
In Gary's mind it was the dominant thing and it would always be the dominant because,

01:01:56.530 --> 01:02:00.010
you know, Bill did languages and Gary did operating systems.

01:02:00.010 --> 01:02:04.410
And he really honestly believed that would never change.

01:02:04.410 --> 01:02:08.410
But what would change the balance of power in this young industry was the characters

01:02:08.410 --> 01:02:10.970
of the two protagonists.

01:02:10.970 --> 01:02:14.810
So I knew Gary back when he was an assistant professor at Monterey Post grad school and

01:02:14.810 --> 01:02:19.550
I was simply grad student and went down, sat in his hot tub,

01:02:19.550 --> 01:02:23.150
smoked dope with him and thoroughly enjoyed it all

01:02:23.150 --> 01:02:25.950
and commiserated and talked nerd stuff.

01:02:25.950 --> 01:02:29.750
He liked playing with gadgets just like Was did and does,

01:02:29.750 --> 01:02:31.910
just like I did and do.

01:02:31.910 --> 01:02:36.710
He wasn't really interested in how you drive the business.

01:02:36.710 --> 01:02:39.750
He worked on projects, things that interest him.

01:02:39.750 --> 01:02:41.870
He didn't go rushing off to the patent office

01:02:41.870 --> 01:02:46.030
and patent CPM and patent every line of code he could.

01:02:47.070 --> 01:02:50.110
He didn't try to just squeeze the last dollar out of it.

01:02:50.110 --> 01:02:53.870
Gary was not a fighter.

01:02:53.870 --> 01:02:55.950
Gary avoided conflict.

01:02:55.950 --> 01:02:59.570
Gary hated conflict.

01:02:59.570 --> 01:03:01.530
Bill, I don't think anyone could say

01:03:01.530 --> 01:03:06.030
backed away from conflict.

01:03:06.030 --> 01:03:09.790
Nobody said future billionaires have to be nice guys.

01:03:09.790 --> 01:03:14.030
Here at the Microsoft Museum is a shrine to Bill's legacy.

01:03:14.030 --> 01:03:16.910
Bill Gates hardly fought his way up from the gutter.

01:03:16.910 --> 01:03:19.270
Raised in a prosperous Seattle household,

01:03:19.270 --> 01:03:21.870
his mother was a homemaker who did charity work,

01:03:21.870 --> 01:03:24.310
his father a successful lawyer.

01:03:24.310 --> 01:03:26.190
But beneath the affluence and comfort

01:03:26.190 --> 01:03:31.270
of a perfect American family, a competitive spirit ran deep.

01:03:31.270 --> 01:03:33.710
I ended up spending the Memorial Day weekend

01:03:33.710 --> 01:03:38.110
with him out on his grandmother's house on Hood Canal.

01:03:38.110 --> 01:03:41.350
She turned everything into a game.

01:03:41.350 --> 01:03:44.510
It was a very, very, very competitive environment.

01:03:44.510 --> 01:03:47.350
And if you spent the weekend there,

01:03:47.350 --> 01:03:48.790
you were part of the competition.

01:03:48.790 --> 01:03:51.750
And it didn't matter whether it was hearts or pickle ball

01:03:51.750 --> 01:03:54.030
or swimming to the dock.

01:03:54.030 --> 01:03:56.110
And there was always a reward for winning,

01:03:56.110 --> 01:03:58.150
and there was always a penalty for losing.

01:03:58.150 --> 01:03:59.470
One time was funny.

01:03:59.470 --> 01:04:01.950
I went to Bill's house, and he really

01:04:01.950 --> 01:04:05.350
wanted to show me his jigsaw puzzle that he was working on.

01:04:05.350 --> 01:04:07.550
And he really wanted to talk about how he did this

01:04:07.550 --> 01:04:09.350
jigsaw puzzle in four minutes.

01:04:09.350 --> 01:04:11.030
And on the box, it said, if you're a genius,

01:04:11.030 --> 01:04:13.470
and you would do the jigsaw puzzle in like seven.

01:04:13.470 --> 01:04:14.470
And he was into it.

01:04:14.470 --> 01:04:15.310
He's like, you know, I can do it.

01:04:15.310 --> 01:04:16.910
I said, don't, you know, I believe you.

01:04:16.910 --> 01:04:18.670
You don't need to break it up and do it for me.

01:04:18.670 --> 01:04:19.510
You know.

