So we just got carried away and said look we can't afford to lose the language business.

01:09:34.730 --> 01:09:35.730
That was the initial thought.

01:09:35.730 --> 01:09:37.650
We can't afford to have IBM not go forward.

01:09:37.650 --> 01:09:40.330
This is the most exciting thing that's going to happen in PCs.

01:09:40.330 --> 01:09:44.810
And we were already out on the limb because we had licensed them not only basic but

01:09:44.810 --> 01:09:50.650
forward-tran, cobalt, assembler, typing tutor, adventure.

01:09:50.650 --> 01:09:55.370
Basically every product the company had we had committed to do for IBM in a very

01:09:55.370 --> 01:09:57.170
short time frame.

01:09:57.170 --> 01:09:58.430
But there was a problem.

01:09:58.430 --> 01:10:03.330
IBM needed an operating system fast and Microsoft didn't have one.

01:10:03.330 --> 01:10:08.490
What they did have was a stroke of luck, the ingredient everyone needs to be a billionaire.

01:10:08.490 --> 01:10:11.770
Unbelievably, the solution was just across town.

01:10:11.770 --> 01:10:16.090
Paul Allen, Gates' programming partner since high school, had found another operating

01:10:16.090 --> 01:10:17.090
system.

01:10:17.090 --> 01:10:21.530
There's a local company here in Seattle called Seattle Computer Products, at that

01:10:21.530 --> 01:10:27.090
Tim Patterson and he had done an operating system very rudimentary operating system that

01:10:27.090 --> 01:10:28.890
was kind of like CPM.

01:10:28.890 --> 01:10:33.210
And we just told IBM, look, we'll go get this operating system from the small local company,

01:10:33.210 --> 01:10:37.330
we'll take care of it, we'll fix it up, and you can still do a PC.

01:10:37.330 --> 01:10:42.810
Tim Patterson's operating system, which saved the deal with IBM, was well adapted from

01:10:42.810 --> 01:10:45.130
Gary Kildall's CPM.

01:10:45.130 --> 01:10:49.610
So I took a CPM manual that I'd gotten from the retail computer store, $5 in 1976

01:10:49.610 --> 01:10:50.610
or something.

01:10:50.610 --> 01:10:57.610
and use that as the basis for the application programming interface, the API for my operating

01:10:57.670 --> 01:11:04.670
system. And so using these ideas that came from different places, I started in April

01:11:04.690 --> 01:11:11.690
and it was about half time for four months before I had my first working version.

01:11:11.690 --> 01:11:16.970
This is it. The operating system Tim Patterson wrote. He called it QDOS, the quick and

01:11:16.970 --> 01:11:23.610
operating system. Microsoft and IBM called it PC-DOS 1.0. And under any name, it looks

01:11:23.610 --> 01:11:29.930
an awful lot like CPM. On this computer here, I have running a PC-DOS and CPM 86. And frankly,

01:11:29.930 --> 01:11:33.370
it's very hard to tell the difference between the two. The command structures are the

01:11:33.370 --> 01:11:37.290
same, so are the directories. In fact, the only obvious external difference is

01:11:37.290 --> 01:11:45.210
the floppy drive is labeled A in PC-DOS and C in CPM. Some difference. And yet,

01:11:45.210 --> 01:11:50.090
generated billions in revenue and the other disappeared. As usual in the PC business, the

01:11:50.090 --> 01:11:54.770
prize didn't go to the inventor but to the exploiter of the invention. In this case, that

01:11:54.770 --> 01:11:58.770
wasn't Gary Kildall. It wasn't even Tim Patterson.

01:11:58.770 --> 01:12:03.730
There was still one problem. Tim Patterson worked for Seattle Computer Products, or

01:12:03.730 --> 01:12:10.370
SCP. They still owned the rights to QDOS, rights that Microsoft had to have.

01:12:10.370 --> 01:12:13.110
Then we went back and said to them,

01:12:13.110 --> 01:12:16.010
look, we want to buy this thing.

01:12:16.010 --> 01:12:19.050
And SCP was like most little companies,

01:12:19.050 --> 01:12:20.650
they always needed cash.

01:12:20.650 --> 01:12:24.610
And so that was when they went into the negotiation.

01:12:24.610 --> 01:12:27.550
And so ended up working out a deal

01:12:27.550 --> 01:12:33.490
to buy the operating system from him for whatever usage

01:12:33.490 --> 01:12:36.170
we wanted for $50,000.

01:12:36.170 --> 01:12:40.050
Hey, let's pause there to savor an historic moment.

01:12:40.050 --> 01:12:45.050
for whatever usage we wanted for $50,000.

01:12:45.050 --> 01:12:48.050
It had to be the deal of the century, if not the millennium.

01:12:48.050 --> 01:12:53.050
It was certainly the deal that made Bill Gates and Paul Allen multi-billionaires

01:12:53.050 --> 01:12:59.050
and allowed Paul Allen to buy toys like these, his own NBA basketball team and arena.

01:12:59.050 --> 01:13:03.050
Microsoft bought outright for $50,000 the operating system they needed,

01:13:03.050 --> 01:13:08.050
and they turned around and licensed it to the world for up to $50 per PC.

01:13:08.050 --> 01:13:14.050
Think of it, 100 million personal computers running MS-DOS software funneling billions into Microsoft.

01:13:14.050 --> 01:13:20.050
A company that back then was 50 kids managed by a 25-year-old who needed to wash his hair.

01:13:20.050 --> 01:13:24.050
Nice work if you can get it. And Microsoft got it.

01:13:24.050 --> 01:13:34.800
There are no two places further apart in the USA than southeastern Florida and Washington State, where Microsoft is staying.

01:13:34.800 --> 01:13:36.800
This is Florida.

01:13:36.800 --> 01:13:38.800
Focke-Ritone, and this building right here

01:13:38.800 --> 01:13:41.800
is where the IBM PC was developed.

01:13:41.800 --> 01:13:47.560
Here the nerds from Seattle joined forces with the suits of corporate America,

01:13:47.560 --> 01:13:52.560
and in that first honeymoon year, they pulled off a fantastic achievement.

01:13:52.560 --> 01:13:56.560
After we got a package in the mail from the people down in Florida.

01:13:56.560 --> 01:14:01.560
As August 1981 approached, the deadline for the launch of the IBM Acorn,

01:14:01.560 --> 01:14:03.560
the PC industry held its breath.

01:14:03.560 --> 01:14:06.400
supposedly maybe at this very moment

01:14:06.400 --> 01:14:09.520
IBM is announcing a personal computer

