to other players in the PC marketplace, which meant trouble in the long run for Big Blue.

01:17:02.460 --> 01:17:08.660
The key to the structure of our deal was that IBM had no control over our licensing to

01:17:08.660 --> 01:17:14.180
other people. The lesson of the computer industry in mainframes was that over time

01:17:14.180 --> 01:17:19.400
people built compatible machines or clones, whatever term you want to use.

01:17:19.400 --> 01:17:24.480
And so really the primary upside on the deal we have with IBM, because they had a fixed

01:17:24.480 --> 01:17:30.720
fee, we got about $80,000 and we got some other money for some special work we did,

01:17:30.720 --> 01:17:34.200
but no royalty from them.

01:17:34.200 --> 01:17:38.040
And that's the DOS and basic as well.

01:17:38.040 --> 01:17:45.380
And so we were hoping a lot of other people would come along and do compatible machines.

01:17:45.380 --> 01:17:51.140
There were other PCs that were sorta like the IBM PC, kinda like it, but IBM now had

01:17:51.140 --> 01:17:55.180
50% market share and was defining what a PC meant.

01:17:55.180 --> 01:17:58.400
What the public wanted was IBM PCs.

01:17:58.400 --> 01:18:05.260
So to be successful, other manufacturers would have to build computers exactly like the IBM.

01:18:05.260 --> 01:18:08.980
They wanted to copy the IBM PC, to clone it.

01:18:08.980 --> 01:18:10.980
How could they do that legally?

01:18:10.980 --> 01:18:31.080
Well, welcome to the world of reverse engineering.

01:18:31.080 --> 01:18:36.280
This is what reverse engineering can get you if you do it right.

01:18:36.280 --> 01:18:41.480
It's the modest Aspen Colorado ski shack of Rod Canyon, one of the founders of Compaq,

01:18:41.480 --> 01:18:45.520
the company set up to compete head on with the IBM PC.

01:18:45.520 --> 01:18:50.560
Back in 1982, Rod and three fellow engineers from Texas Instruments sketched out a computer

01:18:50.560 --> 01:18:55.200
design on a placemat at the House of Pies restaurant in Houston, Texas.

01:18:55.200 --> 01:19:00.040
They decided to manufacture and market a portable version of the IBM PC using the

01:19:00.040 --> 01:19:03.640
curious technique of reverse engineering.

01:19:03.640 --> 01:19:08.820
Reverse engineering is figuring out after something's already been created how it ticks, what

01:19:08.820 --> 01:19:13.200
makes it work, usually for the purpose of creating something that works the same way or at least

01:19:13.200 --> 01:19:21.380
does something like the thing you're trying to reverse engineer.

01:19:21.380 --> 01:19:23.420
Here's how you clone a PC.

01:19:23.420 --> 01:19:25.540
IBM had made it easy to copy.

01:19:25.540 --> 01:19:29.580
The microprocessor was available off the shelf from Intel and the other parts came

01:19:29.580 --> 01:19:31.260
from many sources.

01:19:31.260 --> 01:19:40.580
Only one part was IBM's alone, a vital chip that connected the hardware with the software.

01:19:40.580 --> 01:19:45.740
Called the ROM BIOS, this was IBM's own design, protected by copyright and by Big Blue's

01:19:45.740 --> 01:19:47.860
Army of Lawyers.

01:19:47.860 --> 01:19:51.820
Compact had to somehow copy the chip without breaking the law.

01:19:51.820 --> 01:19:59.180
First, you have to decide how the ROM works.

01:19:59.180 --> 01:20:03.460
So what we had to do was have an engineer sit down with that code and through trial

01:20:03.460 --> 01:20:07.480
an error, write a specification that said, here's how the BIOS ROM needs to work. It

01:20:07.480 --> 01:20:11.060
couldn't be close. It had to be exact. So there was a lot of detail testing that went

01:20:11.060 --> 01:20:15.140
on. You test how that all-important chip behaves

01:20:15.140 --> 01:20:19.820
and make a list of what it has to do. Now it's time to meet my lawyer, Claude.

01:20:19.820 --> 01:20:26.280
I've examined the internals of the ROM BIOS and written this book of specifications.

01:20:26.280 --> 01:20:31.720
Now I need some help because I've done as much as I can do and you need to explain

01:20:31.720 --> 01:20:32.560
What's next?

01:20:32.560 --> 01:20:35.480
Well, the first thing I'm gonna do is I'm gonna go through

01:20:35.480 --> 01:20:37.140
the book of specifications myself,

01:20:37.140 --> 01:20:38.840
but the first thing I can tell you Robert,

01:20:38.840 --> 01:20:39.960
is that you're out of it now.

01:20:39.960 --> 01:20:42.440
You are contaminated, you are dirty.

01:20:42.440 --> 01:20:45.120
You've seen the product that's the original work

01:20:45.120 --> 01:20:47.200
of authorship, you've seen the target product.

01:20:47.200 --> 01:20:49.360
So now from here on in, we're gonna be working

01:20:49.360 --> 01:20:50.520
with people who are not dirty.

01:20:50.520 --> 01:20:52.320
We're gonna be working with so-called virgins

01:20:52.320 --> 01:20:53.920
who are gonna be operating in the clean room.

01:20:53.920 --> 01:20:55.600
I certainly don't qualify there.

01:20:55.600 --> 01:20:56.840
I imagine you don't.

01:20:56.840 --> 01:20:58.360
So what we're gonna do is this.

01:20:58.360 --> 01:21:00.800
We're gonna hire a group of engineers

01:21:00.800 --> 01:21:03.680
who have never seen the IBM Rambias.

01:21:03.680 --> 01:21:05.520
They have never seen it, they've never operated,

01:21:05.520 --> 01:21:07.040
they know nothing about it.

01:21:07.040 --> 01:21:09.240
Have you ever before attempted to disassemble,

01:21:09.240 --> 01:21:13.120
decompile or to in any way, shape, or form reverse engineer

01:21:13.120 --> 01:21:14.480
any IBM equipment?

01:21:14.480 --> 01:21:15.280
Oh, no.

01:21:15.280 --> 01:21:17.120
Have you ever tried to disassemble or?

01:21:17.120 --> 01:21:19.880
This is the Silicon Valley virginity test.

01:21:19.880 --> 01:21:22.360
And good virgins are hard to find.

01:21:22.360 --> 01:21:23.720
You understand that in the event

01:21:23.720 --> 01:21:25.800
that we discover that the information you're providing

01:21:25.800 --> 01:21:27.320
is inaccurate, you're subject to discipline

01:21:27.320 --> 01:21:28.360
by the company.

01:21:28.360 --> 01:21:30.520
And that can include but not be limited to termination

01:21:30.520 --> 01:21:37.020
immediately. After the virgins are deemed intact, they are forbidden contact with the

01:21:37.020 --> 01:21:41.520
outside world while they build a new chip, one that behaves exactly like the one in the

01:21:41.520 --> 01:21:46.800
specification. In Compact's case, it took 15 senior programmers several months and

01:21:46.800 --> 01:21:52.760
cost $1 million to do the reverse engineering. In November 1982, Rod Canyon unveiled the

01:21:52.760 --> 01:21:58.880
result. What I brought today is a Compact portable computer. When Bill Murto, another

01:21:58.880 --> 01:22:05.880
compact founder got a plug on a cable TV show, their selling point was clear, 100% IBM compatibility.

01:22:05.880 --> 01:22:10.080
Turns out that all major popular software runs on the IBM personal computer or the

01:22:10.080 --> 01:22:11.880
compact portable computer.

01:22:11.880 --> 01:22:14.880
That extends through all of the software for IBM.

01:22:14.880 --> 01:22:16.880
Yes, it all works on the compact.

01:22:16.880 --> 01:22:18.880
Compact was an instant hit.

01:22:18.880 --> 01:22:22.880
In their first year on the strength of being exactly like IBM, but a little cheaper,

01:22:22.880 --> 01:22:25.880
they sold 47,000 PCs.

01:22:25.880 --> 01:22:30.680
In our first year of sales, we said an American business record, I guess maybe a world business

01:22:30.680 --> 01:22:31.680
record.

01:22:31.680 --> 01:22:39.600
Our largest first year sales in history was $111 million.

01:22:39.600 --> 01:22:45.440
So Rod Canyon ends up in Aspen, famous for having the most expensive real estate in America.

01:22:45.440 --> 01:22:49.920
And I try not to look envious while Rod tells me which executive jet he plans to

01:22:49.920 --> 01:22:50.920
buy next.

01:22:50.920 --> 01:22:53.040
And finally he picked the Ler 31.

01:22:53.040 --> 01:22:55.840
Now that was a fun airplane.

01:22:55.840 --> 01:23:00.840
Poor Big Blue. Suddenly everybody was cashing in on IBM's success.

01:23:00.840 --> 01:23:05.840
The most obvious winner at first was Intel, maker of the PC's microprocessor chip.

01:23:05.840 --> 01:23:11.840
Intel was selling chips like hotcakes to clone makers and making them smaller, quicker and cheaper.

01:23:11.840 --> 01:23:16.840
This was unheard of. What kind of an industry had Big Blue gotten themselves into?

01:23:16.840 --> 01:23:21.840
Things get less expensive every year. People aren't used to that in general.

01:23:21.840 --> 01:23:23.800
I mean, you buy a new car, you buy one,

01:23:23.800 --> 01:23:24.960
now, four years later, you go buy one,

01:23:24.960 --> 01:23:27.400
it costs more than the one you bought before.

01:23:28.240 --> 01:23:31.160
Here's this magical piece of an industry.

01:23:31.160 --> 01:23:33.640
You go buy one later, it costs less.

01:23:33.640 --> 01:23:35.240
And it does more.

01:23:35.240 --> 01:23:36.840
What a wonderful thing.

01:23:36.840 --> 01:23:38.680
But it causes some funny things to occur

01:23:38.680 --> 01:23:41.240
when you think about an industry.

01:23:41.240 --> 01:23:43.240
An industry where prices are coming down,

01:23:43.240 --> 01:23:44.720
where you have to sell it and use it right now,

01:23:44.720 --> 01:23:51.060
because if you wait later, it's worth less.

01:23:51.060 --> 01:23:54.180
Where compact lead, others soon followed.

01:23:54.180 --> 01:23:56.720
IBM was now facing dozens of rivals.

01:23:56.720 --> 01:23:59.160
Soon to be familiar names began to appear

01:23:59.160 --> 01:24:01.840
like Amstrad, AST, and Dell.

01:24:01.840 --> 01:24:04.880
It was getting spectacularly easy to build a clone.

01:24:04.880 --> 01:24:06.680
You could get everything off the shelf,

01:24:06.680 --> 01:24:10.040
including a guaranteed Virgin rhombias chip.

01:24:10.040 --> 01:24:13.320
Each new clone maker, free of IBM's big overhead,

01:24:13.320 --> 01:24:16.140
took another bite out of Big Blue's business.

01:24:17.240 --> 01:24:19.840
Well, they really hit with a vengeance in 85.

01:24:19.840 --> 01:24:23.400
The prices were going down on the competitive products

01:24:23.400 --> 01:24:26.540
at about 30% every six months.

01:24:27.500 --> 01:24:29.360
Terror would be a good dip.

01:24:29.360 --> 01:24:31.000
That's not a good phrase.

01:24:31.000 --> 01:24:31.840
Terror?

01:24:31.840 --> 01:24:32.680
Oh, of course.

01:24:32.680 --> 01:24:34.880
I mean, we were able to sell a lot of products,

01:24:34.880 --> 01:24:37.320
but it was getting difficult to make money.

01:24:37.320 --> 01:24:38.680
And where did every clone maker

01:24:38.680 --> 01:24:40.280
buy his operating system?

01:24:40.280 --> 01:24:42.000
Microsoft, of course.

01:24:42.000 --> 01:24:44.600
By the mid-80s, it was boom time for Bill.

01:24:44.600 --> 01:24:47.680
The teenage entrepreneur had predicted a PC on every desk

01:24:47.680 --> 01:24:50.560
and in every home running Microsoft software.

01:24:50.560 --> 01:24:52.480
It was actually coming true.

01:24:53.400 --> 01:24:59.400
As Microsoft mushroom, there was no way that Bill Gates could personally dominate thousands of employees.

01:24:59.400 --> 01:25:05.400
But that didn't stop him. He still had a need to be both industry Titan and top programmer.

01:25:05.400 --> 01:25:08.400
So he had to come up with a whole new corporate culture for Microsoft.

01:25:08.400 --> 01:25:14.400
He had to find a way to satisfy both his adolescent need to dominate and his adult need to inspire.

01:25:14.400 --> 01:25:18.400
From the beginning, Microsoft recruited straight out of college.

01:25:18.400 --> 01:25:22.400
They chose people who had no experience of life in other companies.

01:25:22.400 --> 01:25:25.400
In time, they'd be called microsurfs.

01:25:25.400 --> 01:25:32.400
And so a lot of young, I say people, but mostly it was young men, who just were out of school,

01:25:32.400 --> 01:25:40.400
saw him as this incredible role model or leader, almost a guru, I guess,

01:25:40.400 --> 01:25:47.400
and they could sit and spend hours with him, and he valued their contributions.

01:25:47.400 --> 01:25:54.400
and there was just a wonderful camaraderie that seemed to exist between all these young men and Bill

01:25:54.400 --> 01:26:01.400
and the strength that he has and his will and his desire to be the best and to be the winner.

01:26:01.400 --> 01:26:08.120
He is just like a cult leader, really.

01:26:08.120 --> 01:26:12.120
As the frenzied 80s came to a close, IBM reached a watershed.

01:26:12.120 --> 01:26:16.120
They had created an open PC architecture that anyone could copy.

01:26:16.120 --> 01:26:20.940
This was intentional but IBM always thought their inside track would keep them ahead.

01:26:20.940 --> 01:26:21.940
Wrong.

01:26:21.940 --> 01:26:27.120
IBM's glacial pace and high overhead put them at a disadvantage to the leaner clone makers.

01:26:27.120 --> 01:26:31.620
Everything was turning into a nightmare as IBM lost its dominant market share.

01:26:31.620 --> 01:26:36.420
So in a big gamble they staked their PC future to a new system, a new line of computers

01:26:36.420 --> 01:26:42.060
with proprietary closed hardware and their very own operating system.

01:26:42.060 --> 01:26:44.500
It was war.

01:26:44.500 --> 01:26:49.360
planning for operating system 2 today. IBM planned to steal the market from Gates with

01:26:49.360 --> 01:27:00.050
a brand new operating system called drumroll please, OS2. IBM would design OS2 yet they

01:27:00.050 --> 01:27:05.050
asked Microsoft to write the code. Why would Microsoft help create what was intended to

01:27:05.050 --> 01:27:09.650
be the instrument of their own destruction? Because Microsoft knew IBM was the source

01:27:09.650 --> 01:27:14.810
of their success and they would tolerate almost anything to stay close to big blue.

01:27:14.810 --> 01:27:18.810
It was just part of, as we used to call it the time, riding the bear.

01:27:18.810 --> 01:27:24.810
You just had to try to stay on the bear's back and the bear would twist and turn and try to buck you and throw you.

01:27:24.810 --> 01:27:29.810
But, darn, we were going to ride the bear because the bear was the biggest, the most important.

01:27:29.810 --> 01:27:34.810
You just had to be with the bear. Otherwise, you would be under the bear in the computer industry.

01:27:34.810 --> 01:27:37.810
And IBM was the bear. And we were going to ride the back of the bear.

01:27:37.810 --> 01:27:43.810
But it's easy for people to forget how pervasive IBM's influence over this industry was.

01:27:43.810 --> 01:27:48.010
was. When you talk to people who have come into the industry recently, there's no way

01:27:48.010 --> 01:27:53.250
you can get that into their head. That was the environment.

01:27:53.250 --> 01:27:59.210
The relationship between IBM and Microsoft was always a culture clash. IBMers were buttoned

01:27:59.210 --> 01:28:07.210
up organization men. Microsofties were obsessive hackers. With the development of OS 2, the

01:28:07.210 --> 01:28:12.210
strains really began to show. In IBM there's a religion in software that says you have

01:28:12.210 --> 01:28:13.650
have to count K-Locks.

01:28:13.650 --> 01:28:17.010
And a K-Lock is 1,000 line of code.

01:28:17.010 --> 01:28:18.250
How big a project is it?

01:28:18.250 --> 01:28:20.210
Oh, it's a 10 K-Lock project.

01:28:20.210 --> 01:28:22.130
This is a 20 K-Locker.

01:28:22.130 --> 01:28:24.530
And there's a 50 K-Locks.

01:28:24.530 --> 01:28:29.690
And IBM wanted to make it the religion about how we got paid,

01:28:29.690 --> 01:28:31.850
how much money we made off of us, to how much they did.

01:28:31.850 --> 01:28:33.970
How many K-Locks did you do?

01:28:33.970 --> 01:28:35.810
And we kept trying to convince them, hey,

01:28:35.810 --> 01:28:37.810
if we have a developer who's got a good idea

01:28:37.810 --> 01:28:40.570
and he can get something done in four K-Locks,

01:28:40.570 --> 01:28:42.530
instead of 20 K-locks.

01:28:42.530 --> 01:28:44.330
Should we make less money?

01:28:44.330 --> 01:28:47.890
Because he's made something smaller and faster, less clocked.

01:28:47.890 --> 01:28:49.370
Oh, K-locks, K-locks.

01:28:49.370 --> 01:28:51.810
That's the methodology.

01:28:51.810 --> 01:28:56.050
Anyway, it almost makes my back just

01:28:56.050 --> 01:28:59.250
crinkle up at the thought of the whole thing.

01:28:59.250 --> 01:29:01.890
When I took over 89, there was an enormous amount of resources

01:29:01.890 --> 01:29:06.170
working on OS2, both in Microsoft and the IBM company.

01:29:06.170 --> 01:29:09.690
Bill Gates and I met on that several times.

01:29:09.690 --> 01:29:14.030
And we pretty quickly came to the conclusion together that that was not going to be a success

01:29:14.030 --> 01:29:20.960
the way it was being managed.

01:29:20.960 --> 01:29:25.860
It was also pretty clear that the negotiations and the contracts had given most of that control

01:29:25.860 --> 01:29:30.600
to Microsoft's.

01:29:30.600 --> 01:29:33.380
It was no longer just a question of styles.

01:29:33.380 --> 01:29:36.340
There was now a clear conflict of business interest.

01:29:36.340 --> 01:29:39.340
OS2 was planned to undermine the clone market

01:29:39.340 --> 01:29:42.340
where DOS was still Microsoft's major moneymaker.

01:29:42.340 --> 01:29:44.340
Microsoft was DOS,

01:29:44.340 --> 01:29:47.340
but Microsoft was helping develop the opposition.

01:29:47.340 --> 01:29:49.340
Bad idea.

01:29:49.340 --> 01:29:51.340
To keep DOS competitive,

01:29:51.340 --> 01:29:55.340
Gates had been pouring resources into a new program called Windows.

01:29:55.340 --> 01:30:01.340
It was designed to provide a nice user-friendly facade to boring old DOS.

01:30:01.340 --> 01:30:06.340
Selling it was another job for shy retiring Steve Ballmer.

01:30:06.340 --> 01:30:10.340
How much do you think this advanced operating environment is worth?

01:30:10.340 --> 01:30:12.340
Wait just one minute before you answer.

01:30:12.340 --> 01:30:16.340
Watch as Windows integrates Lotus 123 with Miami Vice.

01:30:16.340 --> 01:30:18.340
Now we can take this for a while.

01:30:18.340 --> 01:30:26.340
Just as Bill Gates saw OS 2 as a threat, IBM regarded Windows as another attempt by Microsoft to hold on to the operating system business.

01:30:26.340 --> 01:30:29.340
We created Windows in parallel.

01:30:29.340 --> 01:30:31.660
We kept saying to IBM, hey, Windows is the way to go,

01:30:31.660 --> 01:30:34.220
graphics is the way to go.

01:30:34.220 --> 01:30:35.780
And we got virtually everyone else

01:30:35.780 --> 01:30:37.140
enthused about Windows.

01:30:37.140 --> 01:30:40.900
So that was a divergence that we kept thinking

01:30:40.900 --> 01:30:44.020
we could get IBM to come around on.

01:30:44.020 --> 01:30:47.260
It was clear that IBM had a different vision

01:30:47.260 --> 01:30:48.780
of its relationship with Microsoft

01:30:48.780 --> 01:30:51.440
than Microsoft had of its vision with IBM.

01:30:52.620 --> 01:30:56.220
Is that Microsoft's fault?

01:30:56.220 --> 01:30:59.380
You know, maybe some, but IBM's not blamed

01:30:59.380 --> 01:31:00.620
with there either.

01:31:00.620 --> 01:31:10.910
So I don't view any of that as anything but just poor business and IBM's part.

01:31:10.910 --> 01:31:13.750
Bill Gates is a very disciplined guy.

01:31:13.750 --> 01:31:17.790
He puts aside everything he wants to read and twice a year goes away for secluded reading

01:31:17.790 --> 01:31:18.990
weeks.

01:31:18.990 --> 01:31:23.990
The decisive moment in the Microsoft-IBM relationship came during just such a retreat.

01:31:23.990 --> 01:31:27.870
In front of a log fire, Bill concluded that it was no longer in Microsoft's long-term

01:31:27.870 --> 01:31:30.310
interest to blindly follow IBM.

01:31:30.310 --> 01:31:36.070
If Bill had to choose between OS2, IBM's new operating system, and Windows, he'd choose

01:31:36.070 --> 01:31:37.070
Windows.

01:31:37.070 --> 01:31:39.470
We said, ooh, IBM's probably not going to like this.

01:31:39.470 --> 01:31:41.310
This is going to threaten OS2.

01:31:41.310 --> 01:31:42.310
Now we told them about it.

01:31:42.310 --> 01:31:44.310
Right away we told them about it.

01:31:44.310 --> 01:31:45.310
But we still did it.

01:31:45.310 --> 01:31:46.310
They didn't like it.

01:31:46.310 --> 01:31:48.310
We told them about it.

01:31:48.310 --> 01:31:50.070
We offered to license it to them.

01:31:50.070 --> 01:31:56.270
We always thought the best thing to do is to try and combine IBM promoting the software

01:31:56.270 --> 01:31:59.030
with us doing the engineering.

01:31:59.030 --> 01:32:01.630
So it was only when they broke off communication

01:32:01.630 --> 01:32:04.310
and decided to go their own way that we thought,

01:32:04.310 --> 01:32:06.030
okay, we're on our own.

01:32:06.030 --> 01:32:08.990
And that was definitely very, very scary.

01:32:08.990 --> 01:32:12.390
And we were in a major negotiation in early 1990,

01:32:12.390 --> 01:32:14.270
right before the Windows launch,

01:32:14.270 --> 01:32:16.550
we wanted to have IBM on stage with us

01:32:16.550 --> 01:32:17.950
to launch Windows 3.0,

01:32:17.950 --> 01:32:19.710
but they wouldn't do the kind of deal

01:32:19.710 --> 01:32:22.910
that would allow us to profit.

01:32:22.910 --> 01:32:24.030
It would allow them essentially

01:32:24.030 --> 01:32:25.950
to take over Windows from us,

01:32:25.950 --> 01:32:27.550
and we walked away from the deal.

01:32:27.550 --> 01:32:29.550
which to see you around.

01:32:29.550 --> 01:32:33.190
Jack Samms, who started IBM's relationship with Microsoft

01:32:33.190 --> 01:32:36.110
with that first call to Bill Gates in 1980,

01:32:36.110 --> 01:32:38.990
could only look on as the partnership disintegrated.

01:32:40.070 --> 01:32:43.590
Then they, at that point, I think they agreed to disagree

01:32:43.590 --> 01:32:47.870
on the future progress of OS2 and Windows.

01:32:49.110 --> 01:32:51.870
And internally, we were told,

01:32:51.870 --> 01:32:54.310
thou shalt not ship any more products on Windows.

01:32:54.310 --> 01:33:04.310
And about that time I got the opportunity to take early retirement, so I did.

01:33:04.310 --> 01:33:09.310
Bill's decision by the fireplace ended the 10-year IBM-Microsoft partnership

01:33:09.310 --> 01:33:13.310
and turned IBM into an also-ran in the PC business.

01:33:13.310 --> 01:33:15.310
Did David beat Goliath?

01:33:15.310 --> 01:33:20.310
The Bokeh-Bertone, Florida birthplace of the IBM PC is deserted,

01:33:20.310 --> 01:33:34.610
a casualty of diminishing market share.

01:33:34.610 --> 01:33:40.810
Today IBM is again what it was before, a profitable, dominant mainframe computer company.

01:33:40.810 --> 01:33:43.850
For a while IBM dominated the PC market.

01:33:43.850 --> 01:33:50.330
They legitimized the PC business, created the standards most of us now use and introduced

01:33:50.330 --> 01:33:53.130
the PC to the corporate world.

01:33:53.130 --> 01:33:55.330
But in the end they lost out.

01:33:55.330 --> 01:33:59.530
Maybe it was to a faster, more flexible business culture.

01:33:59.530 --> 01:34:02.050
Or maybe they just threw it away.

01:34:02.050 --> 01:34:07.210
the view of a guy who's been competing with IBM for 20 years, Silicon Valley's most outspoken

01:34:07.210 --> 01:34:10.650
software billionaire, Larry Ellison.

01:34:10.650 --> 01:34:15.570
I think IBM made the single worst mistake in the history of enterprise on Earth.

01:34:15.570 --> 01:34:16.570
Which was?

01:34:16.570 --> 01:34:20.570
Which was the manufacturer, being the first manufacturer and distributor of the Microsoft

01:34:20.570 --> 01:34:24.050
Intel PC, which they mistakenly called the IBM PC.

01:34:24.050 --> 01:34:27.650
I mean, they were the first manufacturer and distributor of that technology.

01:34:27.650 --> 01:34:34.450
I mean, it's just simply astounding that they could basically give a third of their market

01:34:34.450 --> 01:34:40.610
value to Intel and a third of their market value to Microsoft by accident.

01:34:40.610 --> 01:34:45.650
I mean, no one, you know, no one, those two companies today are worth close to, you know,

01:34:45.650 --> 01:34:46.850
approaching $100 billion.

01:34:46.850 --> 01:34:51.730
I mean, not many of us get a chance to make $100 billion mistake.

01:34:51.730 --> 01:34:57.050
As fast as IBM abandons its buildings, Microsoft builds new ones.

01:34:57.050 --> 01:35:01.690
In 1980, IBM was 3,000 times the size of Microsoft.

01:35:01.690 --> 01:35:04.370
Though still a smaller company, today Wall Street says

01:35:04.370 --> 01:35:06.870
Microsoft is worth more.

01:35:06.870 --> 01:35:09.470
Both have faced antitrust investigations about their

01:35:09.470 --> 01:35:11.410
monopoly positions.

01:35:11.410 --> 01:35:15.210
For years, IBM defined successful American corporate

01:35:15.210 --> 01:35:18.970
culture as a machine of ordered bureaucracy.

01:35:18.970 --> 01:35:21.570
Here in the corridors of Microsoft, it's a different

01:35:21.570 --> 01:35:23.910
style, it's personal.

01:35:23.910 --> 01:35:27.070
This company, in its drive, its hunger to succeed,

01:35:27.070 --> 01:35:31.390
is a reflection of one man, its founder, Bill Gates.

01:35:31.390 --> 01:35:34.230
Bill wanted to win.

01:35:35.190 --> 01:35:40.190
Incredible desire to win and to beat other people.

01:35:40.450 --> 01:35:43.590
At Microsoft, the whole idea was that

01:35:43.590 --> 01:35:47.630
we would put people under, you know.

01:35:47.630 --> 01:35:52.630
And unfortunately, that's happened a lot.

01:35:53.910 --> 01:35:56.050
Bill Gates is special.

01:35:56.050 --> 01:35:57.750
He wouldn't have had a Microsoft

01:35:57.750 --> 01:36:01.030
would take a random other person like Gary Kildall.

01:36:01.030 --> 01:36:04.310
On the other hand, Bill Gates was also lucky.

01:36:04.310 --> 01:36:07.670
But Bill Gates knows that, unlike a lot of other people

01:36:07.670 --> 01:36:08.270
in the industry.

01:36:08.270 --> 01:36:09.590
And he's paranoid.

01:36:09.590 --> 01:36:12.550
Every morning he gets up and he doesn't feel secure.

01:36:12.550 --> 01:36:13.990
He feels nervous about this.

01:36:13.990 --> 01:36:14.910
They're trying hard.

01:36:14.910 --> 01:36:16.750
They're not relaxing.

01:36:16.750 --> 01:36:19.070
And that's why they show successful.

01:36:19.070 --> 01:36:21.630
And I remember I was talking to Bill once.

01:36:21.630 --> 01:36:24.510
And I asked him what he feared.

01:36:24.510 --> 01:36:28.070
And he said that he feared growing old,

01:36:28.070 --> 01:36:31.430
because once you're beyond 30, since this was his belief

01:36:31.430 --> 01:36:33.270
at the time, once you're beyond 30,

01:36:33.270 --> 01:36:35.470
you don't have as many good ideas anymore.

01:36:35.470 --> 01:36:37.190
You're not as smart anymore.

01:36:37.190 --> 01:36:40.750
If you just slow down a little bit, who knows who it'll be?

01:36:40.750 --> 01:36:44.070
Probably some company that may not even exist yet.

01:36:44.070 --> 01:36:47.990
But someone else can come in and take the lead.

01:36:47.990 --> 01:36:49.670
I said, well, you're going to age.

01:36:49.670 --> 01:36:50.510
It's going to happen.

01:36:50.510 --> 01:36:51.510
It's kind of inevitable.

01:36:51.510 --> 01:36:52.510
What are you going to do about it?"

01:36:52.510 --> 01:36:55.110
And he said, I'm just going to hire the smartest people.

01:36:55.110 --> 01:36:57.790
And I was going to surround myself with all these smart people.

01:36:57.790 --> 01:37:00.790
And I thought that was kind of interesting.

01:37:00.790 --> 01:37:05.150
It was like, oh, I can't be immortal, but maybe this is the second best and I can buy

01:37:05.150 --> 01:37:08.790
that.

01:37:08.790 --> 01:37:13.710
If you miss what's happening, then the same kind of thing that happened to IBM

01:37:13.710 --> 01:37:17.990
or many other companies could happen to Microsoft very easily.

01:37:17.990 --> 01:37:20.990
So no one's got a guaranteed position in the high technology business.

01:37:20.990 --> 01:37:23.990
And the more you think about, you know, how could we move faster?

01:37:23.990 --> 01:37:24.990
What could we do better?

01:37:24.990 --> 01:37:28.990
Are there good ideas out there that we should be going beyond?

01:37:28.990 --> 01:37:30.990
It's important.

01:37:30.990 --> 01:37:32.990
And I wouldn't trade places with anyone,

01:37:32.990 --> 01:37:41.990
but the reason I like my job so much is we have to constantly stay on top of those things.

01:37:41.990 --> 01:37:49.990
The Windows software system that ended the alliance between Microsoft and IBM pushed Gates past all his rivals.

01:37:49.990 --> 01:37:59.990
Microsoft had been working on the software for years, but it wasn't until 1990 that they finally came up with a version that not only worked properly, it blew their rivals away.

01:37:59.990 --> 01:38:02.990
And where did the idea for this software come from?

01:38:02.990 --> 01:38:04.990
Well, not from Microsoft, of course.

01:38:08.970 --> 01:38:12.970
It came from the hippies at Apple. Lights, camera, boot up.

01:38:12.970 --> 01:38:16.970
In 1984, they made a famous TV commercial.

01:38:16.970 --> 01:38:19.970
Apple had set out to create the first user-friendly PC,

01:38:19.970 --> 01:38:23.970
just as IBM and Microsoft were starting to make a machine for businesses.

01:38:23.970 --> 01:38:27.970
When the TV commercial aired, Apple launched the Macintosh.

01:38:27.970 --> 01:38:35.970
The computer and the commercial were aimed directly at IBM,

01:38:35.970 --> 01:38:38.970
which the kids in Cupertino thought of as Big Brother.

01:38:38.970 --> 01:38:41.970
But Apple had targeted the wrong people.

01:38:41.970 --> 01:39:58.920
Apple. It wasn't Big Brother they should have been worrying about. It was Big Bill Gates.

01:39:58.920 --> 01:40:01.520
The Story So Far

01:40:01.520 --> 01:40:07.320
In 1980, just four years after being founded in a California garage, Apple was the biggest

01:40:07.320 --> 01:40:10.480
maker of PCs in the world.

01:40:10.480 --> 01:40:17.120
Computer giant IBM was not amused and fought back, launching its own PC in 1981.

01:40:17.120 --> 01:40:23.160
So built from copycat technology, IBM's PC was an enormous hit and spawned many imitators,

01:40:23.160 --> 01:40:25.620
the PC clones.

01:40:25.620 --> 01:40:28.160
But PCs were still a pain to use.

01:40:28.160 --> 01:40:31.400
A revolution was needed to make them friendlier.

01:40:31.400 --> 01:41:23.780
Now, view on.

01:41:23.780 --> 01:41:26.980
Welcome to the launch of Windows 95.

01:41:26.980 --> 01:41:30.030
Welcome, Microsofty.

01:41:30.030 --> 01:41:31.030
Nice to have you all here.

01:41:31.030 --> 01:41:32.030
But now let's welcome the chairman of Microsoft.

01:41:32.030 --> 01:41:38.030
This is a man, a man so successful.

01:41:38.030 --> 01:41:41.030
This chauffeur is Ross Perot, ladies and gentlemen.

01:41:41.030 --> 01:41:44.030
Please welcome Bill Gates.

01:41:44.030 --> 01:41:54.600
It's August 24th, 1995, in a suburb of Seattle in the Pacific Northwest.

01:41:54.600 --> 01:41:58.600
This is the biggest, noisiest product launch in the history of the personal computer.

01:41:58.600 --> 01:42:03.600
It's Windows 95 software, and Bill Gates is the star, chairman, chief nerd,

01:42:03.600 --> 01:42:06.600
and spiritual leader of Microsoft.

01:42:06.600 --> 01:42:11.600
This is the latest step in Bill's dream to have his software running on every PC

01:42:11.600 --> 01:42:13.680
every PC in the world.

01:42:13.680 --> 01:42:16.520
We wanted people to be able to appreciate

01:42:16.520 --> 01:42:23.520
how Windows 95 makes computing faster, easier, and more fun.

01:42:23.600 --> 01:42:28.120
In for seven years, it was a lonely, lonely crusade.

01:42:28.120 --> 01:42:29.680
Reality police.

01:42:29.680 --> 01:42:34.440
This moves the whole PC industry up to a whole new level.

01:42:34.440 --> 01:42:35.520
Wait a minute.

01:42:35.520 --> 01:42:37.840
All this publicity is so Bill Gates can claim

01:42:37.840 --> 01:42:40.120
that Windows 95 is the latest and perhaps

01:42:40.120 --> 01:42:43.800
significant improvement in the PC since it was invented.

01:42:43.800 --> 01:42:48.200
He can say that his new operating system makes PCs nicer to look at and easier to use than

01:42:48.200 --> 01:42:49.200
ever before.

01:42:49.200 --> 01:42:51.160
They'll no longer be just for geeks and nerds.

01:42:51.160 --> 01:42:54.360
They'll be so easy to use that even my mother will want one.

01:42:54.360 --> 01:42:55.960
But you know what?

01:42:55.960 --> 01:43:03.990
Most of the ideas in Windows 95 were invented 20 years ago.

01:43:03.990 --> 01:43:08.150
The 20-year journey to this software celebration hasn't been easy.

01:43:08.150 --> 01:43:13.030
It has involved huge gambles, passionate commitment, dramatic setbacks, and required

01:43:13.030 --> 01:43:18.160
the occasional crushing of rivals and allies.

01:43:18.160 --> 01:43:21.200
It's the triumph of Bill Gates commercial vision.

01:43:21.200 --> 01:43:25.760
Success in the marketplace doesn't have to come from innovation or from being the best

01:43:25.760 --> 01:43:30.700
if you have a ruthless ability to exploit your opportunities.

01:43:30.700 --> 01:43:36.240
And the way Microsoft made the PC's graphical user interface its own is a textbook example

01:43:36.240 --> 01:43:41.480
of that ability.

01:43:41.480 --> 01:43:45.320
Time for another cringely crash course in elementary computing.

01:43:45.320 --> 01:43:48.800
In the early days of personal computing, the machines were pretty hard to use.

01:43:48.800 --> 01:43:52.560
In part that's because they were primitive, but it's also because computer guys tend to

01:43:52.560 --> 01:43:55.080
like things that are pretty hard to use.

01:43:55.080 --> 01:44:01.560
This is an IBM PC circa about 1983, and on it I've written a letter to my bank manager

01:44:01.560 --> 01:44:04.200
asking him to back one of my get rich quick schemes.

01:44:04.200 --> 01:44:08.440
I need to file the letter now and let me show you how I do it.

01:44:08.440 --> 01:44:09.920
There will be a test on this.

01:44:09.920 --> 01:44:21.360
OK, the commands are copy C colon backslash quickrich.doc space

01:44:21.360 --> 01:44:28.240
A colon backslash begging and return.

01:44:28.240 --> 01:44:30.320
Well, not very easy to do.

01:44:30.320 --> 01:44:33.840
Here's a Windows PC about 12 years newer,

01:44:33.840 --> 01:44:35.320
and we'll do exactly the same thing.

01:44:35.320 --> 01:44:39.120
I've written a document, quickrich.doc.

01:44:39.120 --> 01:44:44.760
And I put it in the begging file and it, yes, I really do mean to do it.

01:44:44.760 --> 01:44:46.380
And that's it.

01:44:46.380 --> 01:44:51.100
Pictures rather than words, making the PC easy and intuitive.

01:44:51.100 --> 01:44:58.140
This is called a graphical user interface, GUI or GUI, where they come up with these names.

01:44:58.140 --> 01:45:03.260
The battle to bring GUIs to PCs and make them more user friendly took 10 years and

01:45:03.260 --> 01:45:04.940
is a hell of a story.

01:45:04.940 --> 01:45:06.700
That's what this program is about.

01:45:06.700 --> 01:45:11.500
It's also about how Bill Gates ended up master of the gooey universe and a gazillionaire.

01:45:11.500 --> 01:45:15.820
I never said it was a fairy story.

01:45:15.820 --> 01:45:21.700
It all began in 1971 in Palo Alto just south of San Francisco.

01:45:21.700 --> 01:45:29.300
When Xerox, the copier company, set up the Palo Alto Research Center, or PARC.

01:45:29.300 --> 01:45:33.760
Xerox management had a sinking feeling that if people started reading computer screens

01:45:33.760 --> 01:45:37.620
instead of paper, Xerox was in trouble.

01:45:37.620 --> 01:45:43.300
unless they could dominate the paperless office of the future.

01:45:43.300 --> 01:45:48.660
You could take computer technology into the office and make the office a much better place

01:45:48.660 --> 01:45:49.660
to work.

01:45:49.660 --> 01:45:58.980
More productive, more enjoyable, a lot more enjoyable, more interesting, more rewarding.

01:45:58.980 --> 01:46:01.100
And so we set to work on it.

01:46:01.100 --> 01:46:04.220
Bob Taylor ran Park's computer science lab.

01:46:04.220 --> 01:46:08.340
And one of the first things he did was to buy bean bags for his researchers to sit on

01:46:08.340 --> 01:46:09.340
and brainstorm.

01:46:09.340 --> 01:46:12.420
These are a couple of the original bean bag chairs.

01:46:12.420 --> 01:46:15.940
The role of bean bag chair in computer science is ease of use.

01:46:15.940 --> 01:46:16.940
Okay.

01:46:16.940 --> 01:46:22.900
It was said that of the top 100 computer researchers in the world, 58 worked at Park.

01:46:22.900 --> 01:46:26.300
Strange as the staff never exceeded 50.

01:46:26.300 --> 01:46:31.540
But Taylor gave these nerd geniuses unlimited resources and protected them from commercial

01:46:31.540 --> 01:46:32.540
pressures.

01:46:32.540 --> 01:46:36.180
I feel my my neural capacity already increasing.

01:46:36.180 --> 01:46:37.180
Here you go.

01:46:37.180 --> 01:46:38.180
Oh God.

01:46:38.180 --> 01:46:41.980
The atmosphere of Park was electric.

01:46:41.980 --> 01:46:44.580
There was total intellectual freedom.

01:46:44.580 --> 01:46:47.060
There was no conventional wisdom.

01:46:47.060 --> 01:46:50.460
Almost every idea was up for challenge and got challenged regularly.

01:46:50.460 --> 01:46:54.260
The management said, go create the new world.

01:46:54.260 --> 01:46:56.500
We don't understand it.

01:46:56.500 --> 01:46:59.380
Here are people who have a lot of ideas and tremendous talent.

01:46:59.380 --> 01:47:01.380
Young, energetic.

01:47:01.380 --> 01:47:07.700
came there specifically to work on five-year programs that were their dreams.

01:47:07.700 --> 01:47:13.540
This is the computer room in the basement of the Xerox Palo Alto Research Center.

01:47:13.540 --> 01:47:17.980
About 25 years ago, they built the max time-sharing system in here, and now it's loaded with

01:47:17.980 --> 01:47:21.020
all sorts of other computers.

01:47:21.020 --> 01:47:24.620
And there's one that we're really interested in here.

01:47:24.620 --> 01:47:25.620
Let's see.

01:47:25.620 --> 01:47:26.620
Here it is.

01:47:26.620 --> 01:47:29.460
Let me turn on the lights.

01:47:29.460 --> 01:47:31.260
Okay.

01:47:31.260 --> 01:47:37.760
Here we have it. This is a Xerox Alto computer built around 1973. Some people would argue

01:47:37.760 --> 01:47:43.100
that this is the first personal computer. It really isn't because for one thing it wasn't

01:47:43.100 --> 01:47:48.600
ever for sale and the parts alone cost about $10,000 but it has all the elements of quite

01:47:48.600 --> 01:47:52.100
a modern personal computer and without it we wouldn't have the Macintosh, we wouldn't

01:47:52.100 --> 01:47:55.540
have Windows, we wouldn't have most of the things we value in computing today.

01:47:55.540 --> 01:48:00.140
And ironically none of those things has a Xerox name on it.

01:48:00.140 --> 01:48:02.140
That's the mail this morning.

01:48:02.140 --> 01:48:06.580
This promotional film made in the mid-70s to flaunt Xerox Park research

01:48:06.580 --> 01:48:09.580
shows just how revolutionary the alto was.

01:48:09.580 --> 01:48:11.580
It was friendly and intuitive.

01:48:11.580 --> 01:48:13.580
This is an experimental office system.

01:48:13.580 --> 01:48:14.580
It's in use now.

01:48:14.580 --> 01:48:19.280
It had the first GUI using a mouse to point to information on the screen.

01:48:19.280 --> 01:48:22.280
It was linked to other PCs by a system called Ethernet,

01:48:22.280 --> 01:48:24.280
the first computer network.

01:48:24.280 --> 01:48:28.780
And what you saw on the screen was precisely what you got on your laser printer.

01:48:28.780 --> 01:48:31.380
It was way ahead of its time.

01:48:31.380 --> 01:48:33.080
Everybody wanted to make a real difference.

01:48:33.080 --> 01:48:35.280
We really thought we were changing the world

01:48:35.280 --> 01:48:41.680
and that at the end of this project or the set of projects,

01:48:41.680 --> 01:48:44.960
personal computing would burst on the scene exactly the way

01:48:44.960 --> 01:48:50.560
we had envisioned it and take everybody by total surprise.

01:48:50.560 --> 01:48:53.420
But the brilliant researchers at PARC could never persuade

01:48:53.420 --> 01:48:56.220
Xerox management that their vision was accurate.

01:48:56.220 --> 01:48:59.460
Head office in New York ignored the revolutionary technologies.

01:48:59.460 --> 01:49:01.620
They owned 3,000 miles away.

01:49:01.620 --> 01:49:03.980
They just didn't get it.

01:49:03.980 --> 01:49:06.500
And none of the main body of the company

01:49:06.500 --> 01:49:09.740
was prepared to accept the answers.

01:49:09.740 --> 01:49:12.140
So there was a tremendous mismatch

01:49:12.140 --> 01:49:15.700
between the management and what the researchers were doing

01:49:15.700 --> 01:49:18.260
in that these guys had never fantasized

01:49:18.260 --> 01:49:20.700
about what the future of the office was going to be.

01:49:20.700 --> 01:49:22.540
And when it was presented to them,

01:49:22.540 --> 01:49:25.940
they had no mechanisms for turning those ideas

01:49:25.940 --> 01:49:27.340
into real life products.

01:49:27.340 --> 01:49:31.460
And that was really the frustrating part of it

01:49:31.460 --> 01:49:33.380
because you were talking to people

01:49:33.380 --> 01:49:35.340
who didn't understand the vision.

01:49:35.340 --> 01:49:37.620
And yet the vision was getting created every day

01:49:37.620 --> 01:49:40.260
within the Palo Alto Research Center.

01:49:40.260 --> 01:49:45.470
And there was no one to receive that vision.

01:49:45.470 --> 01:49:47.910
But a few miles down the road from Palo Alto

01:49:47.910 --> 01:49:50.190
was a man ready to share the vision.

01:49:51.230 --> 01:49:53.190
The most dangerous man in Silicon Valley

01:49:53.190 --> 01:49:55.350
sits in an office in this building.

01:49:55.350 --> 01:49:56.550
People love him and hate him,

01:49:56.550 --> 01:50:02.310
often at the same time. For ten years by sheer force of will, he made the personal computer

01:50:02.310 --> 01:50:06.950
industry follow his direction. With this guy, we're not talking about someone driven by the

01:50:06.950 --> 01:50:11.430
profit motive and a desire for an opulent retirement at the age of forty. No, we're

01:50:11.430 --> 01:50:16.550
talking holy war, we're talking rivers of blood and fields of dead martyrs to the

01:50:16.550 --> 01:50:20.510
cause of greater computing. We're talking about a guy who sees the personal computer

01:50:20.510 --> 01:50:23.670
as his tool for changing the world.

01:50:23.670 --> 01:50:32.700
We're talking about Steve Jobs.

01:50:32.700 --> 01:50:34.020
Steve Jobs.

01:50:34.020 --> 01:50:36.180
When I wasn't sure what the word charisma meant,

01:50:36.180 --> 01:50:42.800
I met Steve Jobs and then I knew.

01:50:42.800 --> 01:50:45.320
Steve Jobs is on my eternal heroes list.

01:50:45.320 --> 01:50:48.000
There's nothing he can ever do to get off it.

01:50:48.000 --> 01:50:51.960
He wanted you to be great.

01:50:51.960 --> 01:50:55.520
And he wanted you to create something that was great.

01:50:55.520 --> 01:51:01.090
And he was going to make you do that.

01:51:01.090 --> 01:51:04.430
He's also obnoxious.

01:51:04.430 --> 01:51:06.530
And this comes from his high standards.

01:51:06.530 --> 01:51:09.130
He has extremely high standards, and he has no

01:51:09.130 --> 01:51:11.970
patience with people who don't either share those

01:51:11.970 --> 01:51:13.730
standards or perform to them.

01:51:18.000 --> 01:51:21.040
And I'm also one of these people that I don't really

01:51:21.040 --> 01:51:23.540
care about being right.

01:51:23.540 --> 01:51:27.890
I just care about success.

01:51:27.890 --> 01:51:33.210
Steve Jobs had co-founded Apple Computer in 1976.

01:51:33.210 --> 01:51:37.090
The first popular personal computer, the Apple II, was a hit

01:51:37.090 --> 01:51:42.150
and made Steve Jobs one of the biggest names in a brand new industry.

01:51:42.150 --> 01:51:46.250
At the height of Apple's early success in December 1979,

01:51:46.250 --> 01:51:51.930
Jobs, then all of 24, had a privileged invitation to visit Xerox Park.

01:51:51.930 --> 01:51:56.010
And they showed me really three things.

01:51:56.010 --> 01:52:01.770
But I was so blinded by the first one that I didn't even really see the other two.

01:52:01.770 --> 01:52:06.470
One of the things they showed me was object-oriented programming.

01:52:06.470 --> 01:52:09.330
They showed me that, but I didn't even see that.

01:52:09.330 --> 01:52:12.090
The other one they showed me was really a networked computer system.

01:52:12.090 --> 01:52:16.650
They had over 100 alto computers all networked using email, et cetera, et cetera.

01:52:16.650 --> 01:52:18.810
I didn't even see that.

01:52:18.810 --> 01:52:23.050
I was so blinded by the first thing they showed me, which was the graphical user

01:52:23.050 --> 01:52:24.170
interface.

01:52:24.170 --> 01:52:26.930
I thought it was the best thing I had ever seen in my life.

01:52:26.930 --> 01:52:29.610
Now, remember, it was very flawed.

01:52:29.610 --> 01:52:31.510
What we saw was incomplete.

01:52:31.510 --> 01:52:33.310
They'd done a bunch of things wrong.

01:52:33.310 --> 01:52:34.310
But we didn't know that at the time.

01:52:34.310 --> 01:52:40.910
It's still, though, they had the germ of the idea was there and they'd done it very well.

01:52:40.910 --> 01:52:47.130
And within, you know, ten minutes, it was obvious to me that all computers would work

01:52:47.130 --> 01:52:49.170
like this someday.

01:52:49.170 --> 01:52:50.830
It was a turning point.

01:52:50.830 --> 01:52:54.170
Jobs decided this was the way forward for Apple.

01:52:54.170 --> 01:53:02.330
He came back and I almost said ask, but the truth is demanded that his entire programming

01:53:02.330 --> 01:53:07.090
team get a demo of the small talk system.

01:53:07.090 --> 01:53:12.730
And the then head of the science center asked me to give the demo because Steve specifically

01:53:12.730 --> 01:53:14.730
asked for me to give the demo.

01:53:14.730 --> 01:53:16.310
And I said, no way.

01:53:16.310 --> 01:53:22.170
I had a big argument with the Xerox executives telling them that they were about to give

01:53:22.170 --> 01:53:25.610
give away the kitchen sink.

01:53:25.610 --> 01:53:30.170
And I said I would only do it if I were ordered to do it,

01:53:30.170 --> 01:53:32.610
because then of course would be their responsibility,

01:53:32.610 --> 01:53:33.810
and that's what they did.

01:53:33.810 --> 01:53:37.050
The mouse is a pointing device that moves a cursor

01:53:37.050 --> 01:53:39.410
around the display screen.

01:53:39.410 --> 01:53:42.090
Adele and her colleagues showed the Apple programmers

01:53:42.090 --> 01:53:46.050
an auto machine running a graphical user interface.

01:53:46.050 --> 01:53:48.850
A selected window displays above other windows,

01:53:48.850 --> 01:53:50.490
much like placing a piece of paper

01:53:50.490 --> 01:53:52.810
on top of a stack on a desk.

01:53:52.810 --> 01:53:55.050
The visitors from Apple saw a computer

01:53:55.050 --> 01:53:57.810
that was designed to be easy to use,

01:53:57.810 --> 01:54:01.410
a machine that anybody could operate and find friendly,

01:54:01.410 --> 01:54:02.610
even the French.

01:54:02.610 --> 01:54:03.970
Choose one.

01:54:03.970 --> 01:54:08.290
I think mostly what we got in that hour and a half

01:54:08.290 --> 01:54:10.330
was inspiration.

01:54:10.330 --> 01:54:13.770
And basically just sort of a bolstering of our convictions

01:54:13.770 --> 01:54:19.290
that a more graphical way to do things

01:54:19.290 --> 01:54:22.890
would make this business computer more accessible.

01:54:22.890 --> 01:54:26.570
After an hour looking at demos, they understood our technology

01:54:26.570 --> 01:54:30.270
and what it meant more than any Xerox executive understood it

01:54:30.270 --> 01:54:32.290
after years of showing it to them.

01:54:32.290 --> 01:54:33.690
Basically, they were copy or hands.

01:54:33.690 --> 01:54:37.250
It just had no clue about a computer or what it could do.

01:54:37.250 --> 01:54:41.170
And so they just grabbed defeat from the greatest

01:54:41.170 --> 01:54:43.530
victory in the computer industry.

01:54:43.530 --> 01:54:47.770
Xerox could have owned the entire computer industry today.

01:54:47.770 --> 01:54:50.130
could have been, you know, a company ten times its size.

01:54:50.130 --> 01:54:51.730
Could have been IBM.

01:54:51.730 --> 01:54:52.810
Could have been the IBM of the 90s.

01:54:52.810 --> 01:54:55.490
Could have been the Microsoft of the 90s.

01:54:55.490 --> 01:54:58.370
For Steve Jobs, the road to Damascus passed through

01:54:58.370 --> 01:54:59.530
Palo Alto.

01:54:59.530 --> 01:55:02.330
He persuaded the Apple board to invest in technology

01:55:02.330 --> 01:55:04.690
copying what he'd seen at Xerox Park,

01:55:04.690 --> 01:55:06.410
his instrument of change.

01:55:06.410 --> 01:55:09.690
They hired 100 engineers and started developing a new PC

01:55:09.690 --> 01:55:11.290
code named LISA.

01:55:11.290 --> 01:55:12.730
But there were problems.

01:55:12.730 --> 01:55:15.250
The LISA didn't work properly, and its price tag was

01:55:15.250 --> 01:55:17.610
heading toward $10,000.

01:55:17.610 --> 01:55:24.610
way too much for the average PC buyer. Jobs' domineering style drove everyone nuts too,

01:55:24.610 --> 01:55:27.610
so the board ousted him from his own pet project.

01:55:27.610 --> 01:55:32.690
You know, I brooded for a few months. But it was not very long after that that it really

01:55:32.690 --> 01:55:39.730
occurred to me that if we didn't do something here, the Apple II was running out of gas.

01:55:39.730 --> 01:55:44.910
And we needed to do something with this technology fast or else Apple might cease

01:55:44.910 --> 01:55:49.320
to exist as the company that it was.

01:55:49.320 --> 01:55:54.180
Jobs got his answer from Jeff Raskin, Apple employee number 31.

01:55:54.180 --> 01:56:00.720
Raskin's idea was a $600 computer, as easy to use as a toaster, codenamed Macintosh,

01:56:00.720 --> 01:56:03.000
after America's favorite Apple.

01:56:03.000 --> 01:56:08.040
Jobs liked the price, but not Raskin's design ideas, so Steve took over the Macintosh

01:56:08.040 --> 01:56:11.960
project, determined to make it a cheaper Lisa.

01:56:11.960 --> 01:56:16.040
And so I formed a small team to do the Macintosh.

01:56:16.040 --> 01:56:20.920
And we were on a mission from God to save Apple.

01:56:20.920 --> 01:56:26.760
While Jobs pursued his Mac mission, he needed a more orthodox chief executive to run the company,

01:56:26.760 --> 01:56:30.160
a respectable face who could sell to corporate America.

01:56:30.160 --> 01:56:33.160
He chose Pepsi Cola executive John Scully.

01:56:33.160 --> 01:56:34.720
Scully refused.

01:56:34.720 --> 01:56:38.680
Leave Pepsi for a four-year-old company that had been set up in a garage?

01:56:38.680 --> 01:56:40.080
Are you serious?

01:56:40.080 --> 01:56:43.860
But it was hard saying no to Steve Jobs.

01:56:43.860 --> 01:56:49.040
And then he looked up at me and just stared at me with this stare that only Steve Jobs

01:56:49.040 --> 01:56:54.880
has and he said, do you want to sell sugar water for the rest of your life or do you

01:56:54.880 --> 01:56:58.820
want to come with me and change the world?

01:56:58.820 --> 01:57:04.920
And I just sculpt because I knew I would wonder for the rest of my life what I would

01:57:04.920 --> 01:57:08.120
have missed.

01:57:08.120 --> 01:57:13.960
For the young Mac team, average age 21, this was the start of the toughest but most exhilarating

01:57:13.960 --> 01:57:18.320
assignment of their lives, relentlessly driven by Jobs' ego.

01:57:18.320 --> 01:57:19.840
Oh, look at this.

01:57:19.840 --> 01:57:21.840
And who's this fresh-faced young guy here?

01:57:21.840 --> 01:57:23.840
That's me 11 years ago.

01:57:23.840 --> 01:57:25.840
Had more hair, I guess.

01:57:25.840 --> 01:57:27.340
A little thinner.

01:57:27.340 --> 01:57:29.640
Oh, I love these people.

01:57:29.640 --> 01:57:32.960
They're like family to me, really.

01:57:32.960 --> 01:57:36.240
And we were united by this common bond of trying to do this incredible thing with

01:57:36.240 --> 01:57:38.000
Mac.

01:57:38.000 --> 01:57:44.520
wanted the Mac to revolutionize the PC market. So he insisted that the team deliver perfection.

01:57:44.520 --> 01:57:50.120
Steve was upset that the Mac took too long to boot up when you first turned it on. So

01:57:50.120 --> 01:57:54.040
he tried motivating Larry Kenyon by telling him, well, you know how many millions of

01:57:54.040 --> 01:57:57.800
people are going to buy this machine? There's going to be millions of people.

01:57:57.800 --> 01:58:02.600
And let's imagine that you can make it boot five seconds faster. Well, that's five

01:58:02.600 --> 01:58:07.520
seconds times a million every day that's 50 life times.

01:58:07.520 --> 01:58:13.200
You're you if you can shave five seconds off that you're saving 50 lives.

01:58:13.200 --> 01:58:18.080
And so you know it was a nice way of thinking about it and we did get it to go faster.

01:58:18.080 --> 01:58:22.120
And then this is one of the very first Macintosh wire wraps.

01:58:22.120 --> 01:58:24.040
This is wire wrap board number four.

01:58:24.040 --> 01:58:28.140
As the Mac progressed new features were being continually added.

01:58:28.140 --> 01:58:33.380
said the Mac had to be insanely great and pushed his engineers to the limit.

01:58:33.380 --> 01:58:40.150
He had to because by early 1983 Apple was in trouble.

01:58:40.150 --> 01:58:42.510
And this is what was giving Apple such a headache.

01:58:42.510 --> 01:58:45.870
IBM's first PC launched in August 1981.

01:58:45.870 --> 01:58:47.390
It was a runaway success.

01:58:47.390 --> 01:58:51.010
Within a couple of years more than two million units have been shipped, overtaking

01:58:51.010 --> 01:58:56.750
Apple and making Big Blue the biggest player in the market.

01:58:56.750 --> 01:59:01.870
When IBM personal computer owners look for good software, where can they turn?

01:59:01.870 --> 01:59:03.030
To IBM.

01:59:03.030 --> 01:59:06.630
What was driving IBM PC sales was software.

01:59:06.630 --> 01:59:10.790
Business programs, entertainment, productivity.

01:59:10.790 --> 01:59:14.150
But software for an IBM wouldn't run on the Mac.

01:59:14.150 --> 01:59:18.950
If the Macintosh was to succeed, jobs needed killer applications.

01:59:18.950 --> 01:59:23.790
Enter 25-year-old software supremo Bill Gates.

01:59:23.790 --> 01:59:29.110
At that time, his company, Microsoft, had 100 workers and was growing like crazy thanks

01:59:29.110 --> 01:59:33.310
to DOS, the operating system that drove the IBM PC.

01:59:33.310 --> 01:59:36.030
But DOS sure wasn't a gooey.

01:59:36.030 --> 01:59:40.150
Gates and his aggressive number two, Steve Ballmer, were immediately intrigued by the

01:59:40.150 --> 01:59:41.150
Mac.

01:59:41.150 --> 01:59:45.550
Jobs talked to Bill at some industry conference and said, hey, we're doing, I think Lisa

01:59:45.550 --> 01:59:46.910
was sort of in development.

01:59:46.910 --> 01:59:51.550
He said, I'm going to do the graphical interface machine here at Apple, not just that

01:59:51.550 --> 01:59:52.550
Lisa thing.

01:59:52.550 --> 01:59:56.290
I'm gonna do day one the one that's really gonna be the the winner

01:59:57.290 --> 02:00:05.590
While the Mac was being developed job staged an event a parody of a TV game show to whip up enthusiasm among software developers

02:00:05.710 --> 02:00:09.270
And now ladies and gentlemen the Macintosh software dating game

02:00:10.310 --> 02:00:16.630
Jobs got the three top software bosses of the time to sing the Mac's praises one of them was Bill Gates

02:00:17.110 --> 02:00:22.190
Steve didn't realize he was opening the door to the man who proved to be Apple's main rival

02:00:22.550 --> 02:00:26.550
When was your first date with Macintosh?

02:00:26.550 --> 02:00:30.550
We've been working with the Mac for almost two years now.

02:00:30.550 --> 02:00:34.550
And we put some of our really good people on it.

02:00:34.550 --> 02:00:38.550
Even before we finished our work on the IBM PC,

02:00:38.550 --> 02:00:43.550
Steve Jobs came and talked about what he wanted to do.

02:00:43.550 --> 02:00:46.550
That he thought he could do sort of a lease a bit cheaper.

02:00:46.550 --> 02:00:48.550
We said, boy, we'd love to help out.

02:00:48.550 --> 02:00:50.550
The lease had all its own applications.

02:00:50.550 --> 02:00:53.390
But of course, they required a lot of memory.

02:00:53.390 --> 02:00:54.710
And we thought we could do better.

02:00:54.710 --> 02:00:58.550
And so Steve signed a deal with us to actually provide bundled

02:00:58.550 --> 02:01:00.350
applications for the first Mac.

02:01:00.350 --> 02:01:04.350
And so we were big believers in the Mac and what Steve was

02:01:04.350 --> 02:01:06.870
doing there.

02:01:06.870 --> 02:01:09.470
Most people don't remember, but until the Mac, Microsoft was

02:01:09.470 --> 02:01:10.710
not in the applications business.

02:01:10.710 --> 02:01:12.390
It was dominated by Lotus.

02:01:12.390 --> 02:01:15.470
And Microsoft took a big gamble to write for the Mac.

02:01:15.470 --> 02:01:22.790
And so we got started in early 1982 on our Macintosh software

02:01:22.790 --> 02:01:23.710
effort.

02:01:23.710 --> 02:01:25.790
And I think at that point in time,

02:01:25.790 --> 02:01:29.630
it really clicked with Bill that graphic user interface was

02:01:29.630 --> 02:01:33.190
going to be the way of the future.

02:01:33.190 --> 02:01:36.190
But while Bill was having his own gooey revelation,

02:01:36.190 --> 02:01:40.590
jobs believed that Apple's true enemy was IBM.

02:01:40.590 --> 02:01:45.350
Well, Big Blue dominates the entire computer industry.

02:01:45.350 --> 02:01:54.150
The entire information age was George Orwell Wright about 1984.

02:01:54.150 --> 02:02:00.190
Despite Steve Jobs' showmanship, the IBM PC was hurting Apple's business.

02:02:00.190 --> 02:02:06.030
And most pundits considered that Apple was going to be out of business.

02:02:06.030 --> 02:02:11.150
In a few short months, Business Week ran an article on their cover saying it's over,

02:02:11.150 --> 02:02:14.860
IBM is one.

02:02:14.860 --> 02:02:18.100
The Mac team saw themselves as Apple's pirates,

02:02:18.100 --> 02:02:21.260
but the gang was now being called on to save the ship,

02:02:21.260 --> 02:02:24.220
as the Apple II was losing precious market share.

02:02:25.200 --> 02:02:27.400
In the case of the Macintosh team,

02:02:28.340 --> 02:02:31.260
they were behind schedule in getting the Mac out,

02:02:31.260 --> 02:02:33.260
which is not unusual in high technology.

02:02:35.020 --> 02:02:37.100
And so just getting that product to market

02:02:37.100 --> 02:02:43.270
was extremely important.

02:02:43.270 --> 02:02:45.670
After many delays, a date for the launch

02:02:45.670 --> 02:02:47.590
of the Mac was announced.

02:02:47.590 --> 02:02:52.590
The pressure of the deadline was mounting, but Steve was still a perfectionist.

02:02:52.590 --> 02:02:57.590
No design issue was too small and it was never too late to do it right.

02:02:57.590 --> 02:03:01.590
It was a pressure cooker. We were working until we finished.

02:03:01.590 --> 02:03:06.590
We couldn't go to sleep or anything. I was up for three days before in that very last push.

02:03:06.590 --> 02:03:20.950
And finally, just the stars aligned and the last release we made at 6 a.m. that morning.

02:03:20.950 --> 02:03:25.950
It was now all or nothing because Lisa had turned out an expensive flop.

02:03:25.950 --> 02:03:28.950
The fate of the whole company seemed to rest on the launch of the Mac.

02:03:28.950 --> 02:03:33.950
John Scully had even authorized a $15 million advertising campaign

02:03:33.950 --> 02:03:39.950
to coincide with the Mac's public unveiling, January 24th, 1984.

02:03:39.950 --> 02:03:45.950
I remember how nervous Steve was before the introduction of the Macintosh.

02:03:45.950 --> 02:03:50.950
and the rehearsal the night before was a total disaster.

02:03:50.950 --> 02:03:54.950
Nothing seemed to go right. Steve was upset at everybody.

02:03:54.950 --> 02:03:58.950
We wondered how in the world we were going to get through the introduction the following day.

02:03:58.950 --> 02:04:03.950
But when that moment came, Steve was an astro-showman.

02:04:03.950 --> 02:04:12.270
There have only been two milestone products in our industry.

02:04:12.270 --> 02:04:19.270
The Apple II in 1977 and the IBM PC in 1981.

02:04:19.270 --> 02:04:27.270
Today, one year after Lisa, we are introducing the third industry milestone product, Macintosh.

02:04:27.270 --> 02:04:38.250
Many of us have been working on Macintosh for over two years now, and it has turned out insanely great.

02:04:38.250 --> 02:04:45.690
You've just seen some pictures of Macintosh. Now I'd like to show you Macintosh in person.

02:04:45.690 --> 02:05:13.070
The Macintosh was undoubtedly the first affordable personal computer with a genuine graphical user interface.

02:05:13.070 --> 02:05:18.070
It was also the first computer to be a monument to one man's ego.

02:05:18.070 --> 02:05:23.070
Forget the brilliant work done at Xerox Park and the innovations borrowed from the Lisa.

02:05:23.070 --> 02:05:28.070
On the day, only one man was claiming paternity for the Mac.

02:05:28.070 --> 02:05:41.610
So it is with the figure of both right that I introduce a man who's been like a father to me, Steve Jobs.

02:05:41.610 --> 02:05:48.610
I was standing off stage and as he came off he said this is the proudest, happiest moment of my life

02:05:48.610 --> 02:05:53.610
and it was all over his face, it really was, because he had launched a revolution.

02:05:53.610 --> 02:06:08.910
Ultimately it comes down to taste. It comes down to trying to expose yourself to the best things that humans have done.

02:06:08.910 --> 02:06:13.390
and then try to bring those things in to what you're doing.

02:06:13.390 --> 02:06:15.390
I mean Picasso had a saying, he said,

02:06:15.390 --> 02:06:18.390
good artists copy, great artists steal.

02:06:18.390 --> 02:06:22.430
And we have, you know, always been shameless

02:06:22.430 --> 02:06:24.430
about stealing great ideas.

02:06:24.430 --> 02:06:29.930
And I think part of what made the Macintosh grade

02:06:29.930 --> 02:06:33.430
was that the people working on it were musicians

02:06:33.430 --> 02:06:38.430
and poets and artists and zoologists and historians

02:06:38.430 --> 02:06:42.030
who also happened to be the best computer scientists in the world.

02:06:42.030 --> 02:06:46.930
With delusions of grandeur running rampant,

02:06:46.930 --> 02:06:49.830
Apple created a Hollywood style TV commercial.

02:06:49.830 --> 02:06:53.930
It symbolized how the friendly Mac would free us from the Orwellian tyranny

02:06:53.930 --> 02:06:56.730
of clunky IBM PCs.

02:06:56.730 --> 02:06:59.730
Please.

02:06:59.730 --> 02:07:03.330
One more song for you. One more song.

02:07:03.330 --> 02:07:08.230
Despite the hype, by late 1984 the Mac sales were disastrous.

02:07:08.230 --> 02:07:19.010
This is a highly sophisticated office computer.

02:07:19.010 --> 02:07:25.010
In ad after ad, Apple desperately pointed out that the Mac was far easier to use than the IBM PC,

02:07:25.010 --> 02:07:31.010
but it sold for $2,500, a thousand more than the IBM.

02:07:31.010 --> 02:07:38.010
And despite Jobs' best efforts in recruiting software makers like Bill Gates, applications were scarce.

02:07:38.010 --> 02:07:41.010
It didn't do very much. We had Mac paint and Mac write.

02:07:41.010 --> 02:07:43.610
were our only applications.

02:07:43.610 --> 02:07:48.210
And the market started to figure this out.

02:07:48.210 --> 02:07:54.370
By the end of the year, people said, well, maybe the IBM PC

02:07:54.370 --> 02:07:58.530
isn't as easy to use or is not as attractive as the Macintosh,

02:07:58.530 --> 02:08:00.010
but it actually does something which we

02:08:00.010 --> 02:08:01.690
want to be able to do, spreadsheets, work,

02:08:01.690 --> 02:08:03.290
processing, and database.

02:08:03.290 --> 02:08:05.610
And so we started to see the sales of the Mac

02:08:05.610 --> 02:08:09.370
pay a lot towards the end of 1984.

02:08:09.370 --> 02:08:14.610
and that became a problem following year.

02:08:14.610 --> 02:08:19.450
Cringely's third law of personal computing was right again.

02:08:19.450 --> 02:08:25.010
To succeed, a PC must have an application which alone justifies buying the whole box.

02:08:25.010 --> 02:08:27.570
The IBM PC had Lotus 1.2.3.

02:08:27.570 --> 02:08:33.100
The Mac needed its killer application.

02:08:33.100 --> 02:08:36.300
Whizzywig, another bunch of initials from the world of the nerds.

02:08:36.300 --> 02:08:38.220
What you see is what you get.

02:08:38.220 --> 02:08:39.620
So what's the big deal?

02:08:39.620 --> 02:08:43.180
Well, it turns out that it's very hard to print on paper

02:08:43.180 --> 02:08:46.540
exactly the same image that you see on the computer screen.

02:08:46.540 --> 02:08:49.700
80% of our brain is devoted to processing visual data,

02:08:49.700 --> 02:08:51.980
but that's not the same for computers.

02:08:51.980 --> 02:08:54.100
I've been here writing a letter to my mom,

02:08:54.100 --> 02:08:59.780
and I'm signing it Bob in 72-point-times-Roman-Italic

02:08:59.780 --> 02:09:01.780
type, as befitting myself.

02:09:01.780 --> 02:09:04.340
And then when I tell it to print,

02:09:04.340 --> 02:09:06.900
what comes out is a Bob, but certainly not the Bob

02:09:06.900 --> 02:09:14.460
that I'd intended. Until someone invented a way to print exactly what was on the screen,

02:09:14.460 --> 02:09:18.220
GUI would be, well, a lot of hooey.

02:09:18.220 --> 02:09:24.900
Apple's problem was the dot matrix printer. It gave everything a typewriter quality.

02:09:24.900 --> 02:09:30.260
But salvation was at hand, and once again it owed a lot to Xerox PARC. One of PARC's

02:09:30.260 --> 02:09:35.020
former brains, John Warnock, had invented a technology that allowed a laser printer

02:09:35.020 --> 02:09:39.520
to print exactly precisely what was on your screen.

02:09:39.520 --> 02:09:44.260
He started a company called Adobe to market his invention.

02:09:44.260 --> 02:09:46.820
And what we had figured out how to do that no one else had

02:09:46.820 --> 02:09:49.420
figured out how to do is drive laser printers.

02:09:49.420 --> 02:09:52.620
Within two or three weeks, we had canceled our internal

02:09:52.620 --> 02:09:53.700
project.

02:09:53.700 --> 02:09:56.260
A bunch of people wanted to kill me over this, but we did it.

02:09:56.260 --> 02:10:00.300
And I had cut a deal with Adobe to use their software.

02:10:00.300 --> 02:10:06.560
And we bought 19.9% of Adobe at Apple.

02:10:06.560 --> 02:10:17.870
the investment paid off.

02:10:17.870 --> 02:10:20.470
The power of precise laser printed images

02:10:20.470 --> 02:10:23.950
and a user friendly GUI gave birth to a brand new business,

02:10:23.950 --> 02:10:27.070
desktop publishing.

02:10:27.070 --> 02:10:29.950
The spreadsheet had made us all accountants.

02:10:29.950 --> 02:10:31.870
Now using breakthrough software, we

02:10:31.870 --> 02:10:35.510
could create fancy artwork, snappy looking note paper,

02:10:35.510 --> 02:10:38.630
even counterfeit money.

02:10:38.630 --> 02:10:41.150
The Mac had found its killer application

02:10:41.150 --> 02:10:43.550
and would soon become the PC of choice

02:10:43.550 --> 02:10:45.550
for any creative business.

02:10:45.550 --> 02:10:47.550
It changed my life.

02:10:47.550 --> 02:10:51.550
Just that one instant when I picked up the mouse,

02:10:51.550 --> 02:10:56.550
my whole life changed to building a career as a computer artist.

02:10:56.550 --> 02:11:03.630
The success of desktop publishing came too late for Apple's founder.

02:11:03.630 --> 02:11:06.630
In 1985, Mac sales were still flat,

02:11:06.630 --> 02:11:08.630
but Jobs refused to believe the numbers.

02:11:08.630 --> 02:11:12.630
He simply behaved as if the Mac was a hot seller from the start.

02:11:12.630 --> 02:11:18.470
The grandiose plans of what Macintosh were going to be was just so far out of whack

02:11:18.790 --> 02:11:23.810
With the truth of what the product was doing and the truth of what the product was doing was not horrible

02:11:23.810 --> 02:11:25.230
It was salvageable

02:11:25.230 --> 02:11:32.090
But the gap between the two was just so unthinkable that somebody had to do something and that somebody was John Scully

02:11:32.710 --> 02:11:39.050
John Scully whom jobs saw as his own creation presented the board with his strategy to save the company

02:11:39.050 --> 02:11:42.050
The plan did not include Steve Jobs.

02:11:42.050 --> 02:11:47.050
The board had to make a choice, and I said, look, it's Steve's company.

02:11:47.050 --> 02:11:53.050
I was brought in here to help, you know, if you want him to run it, that's fine with me.

02:11:53.050 --> 02:11:59.050
But, you know, we've got to at least decide what we're going to do, and everyone's got to get behind it.

02:11:59.050 --> 02:12:03.050
But he took it as a personal attack, started attacking Scully,

02:12:03.050 --> 02:12:10.050
which backed himself into a corner because he was sure that the board would support him and not Scully.

02:12:10.050 --> 02:12:16.050
And ultimately after the board talked with Steve and talked with me,

02:12:16.050 --> 02:12:22.050
the decision was that we would go forward with my plans and Steve left.

02:12:22.050 --> 02:12:28.900
What can I say? I hired the wrong guy.

02:12:28.900 --> 02:12:30.900
That was Scully?

02:12:30.900 --> 02:12:42.900
And he destroyed everything I'd spent 10 years working for, starting with me, but that wasn't the saddest part.

02:12:42.900 --> 02:12:47.900
I would have gladly left Apple if Apple would have turned out like I'd wanted it to.

02:12:47.900 --> 02:12:54.900
People in the company had very mixed feelings about it. Everyone had been terrorized by Steve Jobs at some point or another,

02:12:54.900 --> 02:13:00.300
So there was a certain relief that the terrorists would be gone.

02:13:00.300 --> 02:13:05.340
And on the other hand, I think there was incredible respect for Steve Jobs by the very same people.

02:13:05.340 --> 02:13:08.660
And we were all very worried what would happen to this company without the visionary,

02:13:08.660 --> 02:13:12.260
without the founder, without the charisma.

02:13:12.260 --> 02:13:14.460
Apple never recovered from losing Steve.

02:13:14.460 --> 02:13:17.580
Steve was the heart and soul in driving force.

02:13:17.580 --> 02:13:20.260
It would be quite a different place today.

02:13:20.260 --> 02:13:32.710
They lost their soul.

02:13:32.710 --> 02:13:37.510
The years after Steve Jobs left were the most profitable for Apple Computer.

02:13:37.510 --> 02:13:42.510
Apple people worked hard, they played hard, they made the computer business look like a beach party.

02:13:42.510 --> 02:13:46.910
The forces of freedom is Macintosh.

02:13:46.910 --> 02:13:52.910
In this bizarre promo to inspire their sales force, Apple stressed that the max ease of use

02:13:52.910 --> 02:13:56.410
could liberate the pathetic prisoners of the IBM PC.

02:13:56.410 --> 02:14:03.910
We'll fight him in the office and the classroom and the desktop with superior weapons.

02:14:03.910 --> 02:14:10.410
With improvements to the hardware and the boom in desktop publishing, Mac production went into overdrive.

02:14:10.410 --> 02:14:15.410
By 1987, Apple was selling a million a year IBM numbers.

02:14:15.410 --> 02:14:19.080
Let's go get them.

02:14:19.080 --> 02:14:26.960
The Mac minted money. Half its $2,000 price was pure profit.

02:14:26.960 --> 02:14:37.180
Macintosh.

02:14:37.180 --> 02:14:43.180
Apple arrogantly assumed their stuff was so good, consumers would always pay a premium for it.

02:14:43.180 --> 02:14:47.390
Big mistake.

02:14:47.390 --> 02:14:50.390
The Mac really ought to have won the battle for the desktop.

02:14:50.390 --> 02:14:56.390
Okay, it was more expensive than an IBM PC, but if what you wanted was a friendly, easy-to-use system,

02:14:56.390 --> 02:15:00.390
and surely everyone wanted that, then this was the only game in town.

02:15:00.390 --> 02:15:02.390
At least that's what the boys at Apple thought.

02:15:02.390 --> 02:15:05.390
But they weren't reckoning on one man, Bill Gates.

02:15:05.390 --> 02:15:12.390
Gates saw that the Max GUI represented a long-term threat to Microsoft's money machine, to DOS,

02:15:13.190 --> 02:15:18.750
the clunky operating system that sat inside every IBM PC. So Bill had his boys create

02:15:18.750 --> 02:15:24.630
a GUI that sat on top of DOS rather like building a fancy facade on an old building. They called

02:15:24.630 --> 02:15:28.870
it Windows and it wasn't much at first, but it was good enough to defend the DOS

02:15:28.870 --> 02:15:30.470
franchise.

02:15:30.470 --> 02:15:37.070
February or March of 1984, which was just right after the Apple Macintosh had been introduced.

02:15:37.070 --> 02:15:42.110
And at that point in time, we were firmly convinced that we needed to bet on graphic

02:15:42.110 --> 02:15:49.070
user interface, first with the Macintosh and then with Windows.

02:15:49.070 --> 02:15:54.630
At Microsoft, it was a long and often frustrating struggle to find a gooey solution that challenged

02:15:54.630 --> 02:15:55.630
the Mac.

02:15:55.630 --> 02:16:04.160
I know the feeling.

02:16:04.160 --> 02:16:09.560
For years, teams of Microsofties slaved in their windowless offices to build windows,

02:16:09.560 --> 02:16:14.040
refreshed by an endless supply of free sodas.

02:16:14.040 --> 02:16:18.200
And I was the development manager for Windows 1.0.

02:16:18.200 --> 02:16:23.040
We kept slogging and slogging and took us, I don't know, seven versions, but there's

02:16:23.040 --> 02:16:27.920
few versions to get things right before 1990, that's right.

02:16:27.920 --> 02:16:32.280
Windows may at first have been a joke compared to the Mac, but Gates is persistent.

02:16:32.280 --> 02:16:36.320
Slowly it got better and the guys at Apple got worried.

02:16:36.320 --> 02:16:38.960
As each new feature appeared on the Windows GUI,

02:16:38.960 --> 02:16:41.240
the more they thought Microsoft was copying

02:16:41.240 --> 02:16:43.440
the features on the Mac.

02:16:43.440 --> 02:16:46.080
So finally, they sued Microsoft,

02:16:46.080 --> 02:16:48.360
accusing them in a long legal battle

02:16:48.360 --> 02:16:53.160
of stealing the look and feel of Apple's GUI.

02:16:53.160 --> 02:16:56.640
The look and feel, which is how it looks,

02:16:56.640 --> 02:17:00.680
the experience of using it was not patentable,

02:17:00.680 --> 02:17:02.640
but it was copyrightable.

02:17:02.640 --> 02:17:04.140
but there was no precedent law,

02:17:04.140 --> 02:17:06.340
this was going to be a precedent setting case.

02:17:06.340 --> 02:17:08.540
But it was a period of five years

02:17:08.540 --> 02:17:13.260
where, you know, Microsoft's, our whole strategy

02:17:13.260 --> 02:17:17.460
would have been ruined because windows was very important to us.

02:17:17.460 --> 02:17:19.260
They weren't gonna change anything.

02:17:20.260 --> 02:17:25.260
And they were gonna get us to cave in

02:17:26.020 --> 02:17:28.500
or take us all the way to the Supreme Court on this thing.

02:17:28.500 --> 02:17:31.380
We assumed that the lawyers, the judges

02:17:31.380 --> 02:17:35.700
would all come to the right conclusion, which eventually they did.

02:17:35.700 --> 02:17:45.300
An apple lost, but in that period of about six years that this case was going on, it may

02:17:45.300 --> 02:17:51.700
have lulled us into a bit of complacency, thinking that we were going to be insulated

02:17:51.700 --> 02:18:00.740
from the Windows attack.

02:18:00.740 --> 02:18:05.540
The launch of Windows 3 in 1990 killed off Apple's hopes that the Macintosh would win

02:18:05.540 --> 02:18:06.540
the GUI wars.

02:18:06.540 --> 02:18:16.600
Today we're introducing Microsoft Windows version 3.

02:18:16.600 --> 02:18:25.600
The six-year labor to produce a GUI that made IBM PCs and all the clones as easy to use as the Mac finally came up Trump's.

02:18:25.600 --> 02:18:31.600
In a year, Windows 3 sold close to 30 million copies, consigning the Mac to a niche in the market.

02:18:31.600 --> 02:18:38.100
Ladies and gentlemen, the Windows 3 development team.

02:18:38.100 --> 02:18:40.600
Bill Gates' strategy won out.

02:18:40.600 --> 02:18:42.800
At every stage in the PC's development,

02:18:42.800 --> 02:18:44.800
he joined the leading hardware company

02:18:44.800 --> 02:18:48.100
and by carving out a dominant market share for his product,

02:18:48.100 --> 02:18:51.100
made his software the industry standard.

02:18:51.100 --> 02:18:54.600
You know, the original PC did our evangelism

02:18:54.600 --> 02:18:56.600
in the way we created tools for that

02:18:56.600 --> 02:18:58.600
and pulled that together.

02:18:58.600 --> 02:19:01.100
Take Windows. Did we bet our company on that?

02:19:01.100 --> 02:19:02.600
Did that come together?

02:19:02.600 --> 02:19:04.100
Virtually everything we've done

02:19:04.100 --> 02:19:05.100
when we first came out with it,

02:19:05.100 --> 02:19:06.600
there's a lot of skepticism.

02:19:06.600 --> 02:19:13.600
But most of the things we really stuck with them, and despite all that second guessing,

02:19:13.600 --> 02:19:15.040
we're able to pull them off.

02:19:15.040 --> 02:19:20.320
The problem was the industry wasn't measured by who has the best selling personal computer

02:19:20.320 --> 02:19:22.520
or who has the most innovative technology.

02:19:22.520 --> 02:19:28.160
The industry was measured by who had the most open system that was adopted by the

02:19:28.160 --> 02:19:30.240
most other companies.

02:19:30.240 --> 02:19:34.720
And the Microsoft strategy ultimately turned out to be the better business strategy.

02:19:34.720 --> 02:19:38.320
The only problem with Microsoft is they just have no taste.

02:19:38.320 --> 02:19:40.320
They have absolutely no taste.

02:19:40.320 --> 02:19:44.440
And what that means is, I don't mean that in a small way, I mean that in a big way, in

02:19:44.440 --> 02:19:56.760
the sense that they don't think of original ideas and they don't bring much culture

02:19:56.760 --> 02:19:59.600
into their product.

02:19:59.600 --> 02:20:01.080
And you say, well, why is that important?

02:20:01.080 --> 02:20:05.200
Well, proportionally spaced fonts come from type setting and beautiful books.

02:20:05.200 --> 02:20:07.000
That's where one gets the idea.

02:20:07.000 --> 02:20:12.400
If it weren't for the Mac, they would never have that in their products.

02:20:12.400 --> 02:20:18.720
And so I guess I am saddened not by Microsoft's success.

02:20:18.720 --> 02:20:21.120
I have no problem with their success.

02:20:21.120 --> 02:20:24.480
They've earned their success for the most part.

02:20:24.480 --> 02:20:29.120
I have a problem with the fact that they just make really third-rate products.

02:20:29.120 --> 02:20:35.120
I will admit, quite frankly, that I think Windows today is probably four years behind,

02:20:35.120 --> 02:20:41.760
three years behind where it would have been had we not danced with IBM for so long because

02:20:41.760 --> 02:20:49.360
the amount of split energy, split work, split IQ in the company really cost our end customer

02:20:49.360 --> 02:20:52.360
real innovation in our product line.

02:20:52.360 --> 02:20:58.120
And so whenever I hear these criticisms, which I got to say sting sometimes, I say

02:20:58.120 --> 02:21:02.640
to myself, just you watch, just you watch Windows 95, Windows 95.

02:21:02.640 --> 02:21:03.640
There's no lack of focus.

02:21:03.640 --> 02:21:07.960
There hasn't been here for the last three, four years since we didn't have this big split

02:21:07.960 --> 02:21:09.880
with IBM.

02:21:09.880 --> 02:21:14.680
And I think even in the operating systems area, now you'll start to see clear, clear, and

02:21:14.680 --> 02:21:18.520
people will recognize clear leadership.

02:21:18.520 --> 02:21:20.080
We just keep making them better.

02:21:20.080 --> 02:21:22.320
We get millions of phone calls.

02:21:22.320 --> 02:21:25.040
We get to go out there and talk to customers.

02:21:25.040 --> 02:21:26.760
There's nothing cast in concrete.

02:21:26.760 --> 02:21:30.520
If people decide there's something that we should change, we change it.

02:21:30.520 --> 02:21:37.100
a lot better than most industries in that sense. I think the way that application user interfaces

02:21:37.100 --> 02:21:43.160
have advanced over the last decade, Microsoft has been at the forefront of a very high percentage

02:21:43.160 --> 02:21:50.080
of that. And I think it's great stuff.

02:21:50.080 --> 02:21:56.440
On August 24th, 1995, Gates delivered the Clutegross to his software rivals. Windows

02:21:56.440 --> 02:22:02.360
95 combines the PC's operating system and its graphical interface into one package.

02:22:02.360 --> 02:22:08.120
The worldwide promotional campaign costing $300 million, it looks set to become the industry

02:22:08.120 --> 02:22:16.630
standard, supplanting Microsoft's old warhorse, DOS.

02:22:16.630 --> 02:22:25.270
Cue the triumph of Bill, a software nerd is the richest man in the world.

02:22:25.270 --> 02:22:31.870
But even as Bill Gates distrides the PC world like a colossus, ahead lie bigger battles.

02:22:31.870 --> 02:22:35.710
Battles that will make the trouncing of the Mac and mastering the IBM PC look like

02:22:35.710 --> 02:22:42.340
a teapot.

02:22:42.340 --> 02:22:48.100
The Gates fortune was built on setting the industry standard for PC operating systems.

02:22:48.100 --> 02:22:54.040
Fine as long as PCs are standalone boxes on your desk.

02:22:54.040 --> 02:23:00.520
But now they are being linked into a worldwide network, the much-hyped information superhighway.

02:23:00.520 --> 02:23:04.920
The PC on the Internet is a mailbox, a telephone and a television.

02:23:04.920 --> 02:23:09.960
Of course at the center of this will be the idea of digital convergence that is

02:23:09.960 --> 02:23:18.080
taking all the information, books, art, movies, and being able to provide them on demand on

02:23:18.080 --> 02:23:20.600
what the PC will evolve into.

02:23:20.600 --> 02:23:25.520
The Internet is the next wave of the information revolution where there is as yet no industry

02:23:25.520 --> 02:23:29.240
standard, a world where even Bill Gates seems unsure.

02:23:29.240 --> 02:23:33.800
You know, if you take the way the Internet is changing month by month, if somebody

02:23:33.800 --> 02:23:36.720
can predict what's going to happen three months from now, nine months from now,

02:23:36.720 --> 02:23:42.740
today my hands off to them. I think we've got a phenomena here that is moving so rapidly

02:23:42.740 --> 02:23:52.200
that nobody knows exactly where it will go. Bill Gates is interesting on his laurels.

02:23:52.200 --> 02:23:57.820
He's making new alliances like investing in Steven Spielberg's new movie studio DreamWorks.

02:23:57.820 --> 02:24:01.360
It would be silly if we were going to get into the interactive business. He's in

02:24:01.360 --> 02:24:08.960
cable TV with broadcaster NBC and in competition with Rupert Murdoch and Mickey Mouse.

02:24:08.960 --> 02:24:13.760
These tycoons are a far cry from the nerds Bill has so far outsmarted.

02:24:13.760 --> 02:24:17.600
Guys like Gary Kildall who became businessmen by accident.

02:24:17.600 --> 02:24:22.560
Even Bill's victory over IBM was really with a corporate outpost a long way from the attention

02:24:22.560 --> 02:24:27.950
of big blue headquarters.

02:24:27.950 --> 02:24:32.150
No, Bill's new rivals are hotshots, not hippies.

02:24:32.150 --> 02:24:34.270
And one of them is the guy I'm visiting.

02:24:34.270 --> 02:24:38.310
He hopes the internet will go somewhere other than to Bill Gates' bottom line.

02:24:38.310 --> 02:24:44.510
betting it will soon consign the PC itself to the trash can and do the same to Microsoft.

02:24:44.510 --> 02:24:55.240
Bob Pringley, you see Larry Ellison.

02:24:55.240 --> 02:25:00.760
Larry Ellison is the boss of Oracle, a booming business that sells software to companies

02:25:00.760 --> 02:25:03.320
who share information among hundreds of users.

02:25:03.320 --> 02:25:05.760
This is my favorite fish, Halloween fish.

02:25:05.760 --> 02:25:10.240
In Atherton, the most exclusive suburb in Silicon Valley, the bachelor billionaire

02:25:10.240 --> 02:25:15.160
has built himself a $10 million Samurai mansion, naturally.

02:25:15.160 --> 02:25:19.760
I want to have a large pond by five acres of water surrounded by several little buildings

02:25:19.760 --> 02:25:20.760
like a village.

02:25:20.760 --> 02:25:27.280
Oh, with his ceremonial car, Larry contemplates the coming battle with Microsoft.

02:25:27.280 --> 02:25:30.880
People make a terrible mistake of thinking IBM is the present and Microsoft is the future.

02:25:30.880 --> 02:25:35.120
I think IBM is the past and Microsoft is the present and the future has not happened

02:25:35.120 --> 02:25:39.520
so we don't know what company, what technology is going to be dominant.

02:25:39.520 --> 02:25:43.680
These are temple guardians from the Kamakura period.

02:25:43.680 --> 02:25:48.200
And they, you know, you'd have one on either side of your door and the job was to scare

02:25:48.200 --> 02:25:51.800
employees of Microsoft away and keep them from entering the temple.

02:25:51.800 --> 02:25:56.040
We shouldn't spend all of our time wringing our hands about, you know, Microsoft, you

02:25:56.040 --> 02:26:01.400
know, Microsoft World Domination, that there's still room left for innovation.

02:26:01.400 --> 02:26:05.320
There's going to be change and Microsoft's future is not assured.

02:26:05.320 --> 02:26:06.320
That's all.

02:26:06.320 --> 02:26:09.200
Well, anything good for the Internet, you know, we're very supportive of.

02:26:09.200 --> 02:26:11.600
because the internet does not require a PC.

02:26:11.600 --> 02:26:17.500
Larry believes the PC will be replaced with a cheap device he calls an information appliance.

02:26:17.500 --> 02:26:22.480
It will be a glorified television which will access information and computing simply by connecting

02:26:22.480 --> 02:26:25.300
to giant computers via the internet.

02:26:25.300 --> 02:26:30.540
Just like turning on a tap and the PC will go the way of the well and the bucket.

02:26:30.540 --> 02:26:33.640
I hate the PC with the passion.

02:26:33.640 --> 02:26:37.820
Me going down to the store and buying Windows 95, I get in my car, drive down to the

02:26:37.820 --> 02:26:45.020
store by a box, cardboard box full of bits encoded on a piece of plastic, a CD-ROM, bring

02:26:45.020 --> 02:26:49.540
it home, read a manual and install this thing, you must be kidding.

02:26:49.540 --> 02:26:51.660
Put the stuff on the net.

02:26:51.660 --> 02:26:52.660
It's bits.

02:26:52.660 --> 02:26:54.760
Don't put bits in cardboard.

02:26:54.760 --> 02:26:55.860
Cardboard in trucks.

02:26:55.860 --> 02:26:56.860
Trucks to stores.

02:26:56.860 --> 02:26:58.860
Me go to the store.

02:26:58.860 --> 02:26:59.860
Pick this stuff out.

02:26:59.860 --> 02:27:00.860
It's insane.

02:27:00.860 --> 02:27:03.860
Okay, I love the internet.

02:27:03.860 --> 02:27:05.860
I want information.

02:27:05.860 --> 02:27:07.860
it flows across the wire.

02:27:07.860 --> 02:27:10.860
So the way ahead is wired.

02:27:10.860 --> 02:27:13.860
Larry, Bill, everybody agrees on that.

02:27:13.860 --> 02:27:16.860
And we have the nerds of the 70s to thank for making it possible,

02:27:16.860 --> 02:27:19.860
whether the PC itself survives or not.

02:27:19.860 --> 02:27:21.860
As we take up their challenge,

02:27:21.860 --> 02:27:24.860
it's worth finding out how these pioneers made out.

02:27:24.860 --> 02:27:29.860
Steve Jobs sold all his Apple stock in disgust when he was fired,

02:27:29.860 --> 02:27:33.860
but has made another fortune from his stake in a movie animation studio.

02:27:33.860 --> 02:27:37.860
He has no doubts about his contribution to humanity.

02:27:37.860 --> 02:27:40.860
If you talk to people that use the Macintosh, they love it.

02:27:40.860 --> 02:27:44.860
You don't hear people loving products very often.

02:27:44.860 --> 02:27:47.860
You know, really. But you could feel it in there.

02:27:47.860 --> 02:27:50.860
There was something really wonderful there.

02:27:50.860 --> 02:27:56.860
Apple, the company jobs took from a garage to the Fortune 500, is in trouble.

02:27:56.860 --> 02:27:59.860
It is now a fading force in the PC marketplace.

02:27:59.860 --> 02:28:07.280
Apple's other millionaire founder Steve Wozniak spends much of his time teaching computing

02:28:07.280 --> 02:28:11.860
to 11 and 12 year olds.

02:28:11.860 --> 02:28:19.780
IBM created the mass market for the PC but no longer sets industry standards.

02:28:19.780 --> 02:28:26.500
And most of the guys who built IBM's first PC have left Big Blue.

02:28:26.500 --> 02:28:34.190
And Ed Roberts who built the Altair, the very first PC, he turned his back on computing

02:28:34.190 --> 02:28:38.500
and return to his first love, medicine.

02:28:38.500 --> 02:28:41.300
Funny, isn't it, how things turn out?

02:28:41.300 --> 02:28:43.100
After all, the first PC revolution

02:28:43.100 --> 02:28:45.300
caught us all pretty much by surprise.

02:28:45.300 --> 02:28:47.900
Even Microsoft, with 2,000 millionaires

02:28:47.900 --> 02:28:49.300
and at least 2 billionaires,

02:28:49.300 --> 02:28:54.100
never expected to be as successful as they are today.

02:28:54.100 --> 02:28:57.600
Cringley's Universal Law says society takes 30 years

02:28:57.600 --> 02:29:00.300
to adapt new technology into daily life.

02:29:00.300 --> 02:29:03.300
The phone, movies, even television took that long

02:29:03.300 --> 02:29:06.660
before our rear ends became outshaked.

02:29:06.660 --> 02:29:09.740
So far the PC has had 20 years.

02:29:09.740 --> 02:29:11.060
So what comes next?

02:29:11.060 --> 02:29:12.700
Well I'm off to find out.

02:29:12.700 --> 02:29:15.380
See you in 10 years.
